"p": "We tend to assume that the question ‘Will it be me?’ must always have a determinate answer—that in any imaginable case of fission, gradual replacement or memory loss, there is a fact about whether the later person is identical with the earlier one. Puzzle cases are designed to unsettle this assumption, and they succeed less by revealing the fact than by revealing how little turns on it. If my psychological life were continued, in full, by two later people, neither could claim to be me more than the other, yet it would be strange to regard this as equivalent to my death. What I care about in caring about my future—the persistence of memories, intentions and character—appears to survive even where identity, strictly construed, fails. The unsettling suggestion is not that persons do not exist, but that the relation we prize may come in degrees and be separable from the all-or-nothing relation of identity on which our concern was supposed to rest.",
