"p": "The replication crisis is usually narrated as a story about statistical malpractice: small samples, flexible analyses, a publication system that rewarded surprising results. That story is accurate as far as it goes, but its remedy—larger samples, pre-registered analyses—presupposes that the measured quantities were the right ones and merely needed estimating more precisely. A subtler difficulty concerns the bridge between a psychological construct and the operation used to capture it. When ‘self-control’ is indexed by persistence at an unsolvable puzzle, or ‘prejudice’ by milliseconds of reaction time, a precise and replicable result may still tell us little about the phenomenon named in the paper’s title. Statistical reform can make such findings more reliable without making them more meaningful. The more uncomfortable lesson is that a discipline can repair its inferential machinery and remain uncertain whether that machinery is pointed at anything of consequence.",
