"p": "Accounts of early modern religious upheaval have often treated the printing press as an agent in its own right, as though the multiplication of pamphlets sufficed to carry heterodox ideas into households. The difficulty is that the households in question were overwhelmingly unlettered. Recent scholarship has accordingly redirected attention to the circuits through which printed matter was voiced: sermons glossing tracts, ballads setting polemic to familiar tunes, readings aloud in workshops and inns. In such settings the text was not so much received as performed, and performance introduced selection, emphasis and local inflection that no author controlled. This does not demote print; the sheer volume of material available for performance was without precedent. But it relocates the press’s influence from the act of reading to the act of re-voicing, and implies that the reformation of belief was negotiated in innumerable acts of oral mediation whose content the surviving pamphlets record only imperfectly.",
