"p": "A scientific literature is usually imagined as a cumulative record: each study adds a measurement, and the aggregate converges on the truth. The image holds only if the probability that a study enters the record is independent of what it found. Once journals, referees and authors themselves favour results that cross a threshold of significance, that independence fails, and the literature becomes a filtered sample whose mean is displaced in the direction of the filter. No individual in this system need act dishonestly; each preference, taken alone, is defensible. The distortion is an emergent property of many locally reasonable choices. It follows that meta-analysis, which pools published estimates in the hope that errors cancel, can compound the problem rather than cure it, since the errors introduced by selection do not cancel but accumulate in a single direction. Aggregation is a remedy for noise, not for bias.",
