"p": "The metaphor of ‘technical debt’ was coined to make a point that engineers struggled to convey to managers: that expedient code is a loan against future productivity, repaid with interest each time the code must be modified. The metaphor succeeded so well that it now licenses a complacency its originator did not intend. Financial debt is legible; its principal, rate and term are written down, and a borrower can decide rationally whether to refinance. Technical debt has none of these properties. Its principal is distributed across thousands of small decisions, its rate varies with whatever the business later asks of the system, and its term is indefinite. To call a shortcut ‘debt’ is therefore to borrow the prestige of a calculable instrument for a liability that resists calculation. The metaphor remains useful as an argument for restraint, but as a basis for planning it flatters the very ignorance it was meant to dispel.",
