"p": "Restoration ecology long oriented itself by the notion of a baseline: a historical state of an ecosystem, prior to human disturbance, against which damage could be measured and toward which repair could aim. The difficulty is not merely that baselines are hard to establish, though they are; it is that the choice among them is underdetermined by ecological fact. The landscape before industrial agriculture, before medieval clearance, before the extinction of the great herbivores—each is equally ‘natural’, and each implies a different programme of intervention. Newer approaches that abandon fixed targets in favour of restoring processes, letting grazing, flooding and predation shape whatever assemblage results, appear to sidestep the problem. Yet deciding which processes to restore, and at what intensity, reintroduces precisely the evaluative judgement the shift was meant to avoid. The appeal to nature does not remove human choice from conservation; it can only obscure where that choice is being made.",
