"p": "Debates about cleaning old paintings are often framed as a contest between fidelity and sentiment: conservators claim to recover the artist’s colours, while critics mourn a patina that generations have learned to love. The framing concedes too much to the conservators. It assumes that beneath the accretions lies an original state that removal simply discloses, whereas the materials themselves have altered—pigments have shifted, glazes thinned—so that what emerges is not the painting as first seen but a new condition never before visible. Yet the critics’ position is no more secure, for the darkened surface is equally the product of chemistry and chance, not of any intention. Neither intervention nor abstention, then, returns us to an origin; each produces a version of the work under present conditions. The question worth arguing is not which treatment is faithful, but which of these present versions we have reason to want.",
