"p": "Criticism has tended to treat the unreliable narrator as an epistemological device: the text plants discrepancies, the attentive reader detects them, and a corrected account of events is assembled behind the narrator’s back. This model flatters the reader, casting interpretation as successful detection, and it presupposes that the novel possesses a determinate sequence of events against which the narration can be measured. Some of the most disquieting examples of the form withhold exactly this. Their discrepancies do not converge on a single alternative account; they proliferate, so that each correction invites another. In such texts unreliability ceases to be a property of the narrator and becomes a condition of reading itself, and the reader’s confident reconstructions are exposed as further narrations, no more authoritative than the one they replaced. The effect is less to reveal a hidden truth than to implicate the reader in the activity of unreliable telling.",
