"p": "Advocacy for endangered languages has relied heavily on an argument from loss: each language encodes distinctive knowledge—taxonomies of plants, grammatical categories, ways of partitioning time—and its disappearance impoverishes human understanding. The argument is sincere but strategically double-edged. By locating a language’s value in what it can teach outsiders, it implies that documentation, the recording of that knowledge for scholarly use, adequately answers the threat. Communities that have watched their languages archived while their children ceased to speak them have reason to be wary of that implication. Languages do not usually die from neglect but from decisions made by speakers under pressure—parents judging, often accurately, that another language offers their children better prospects. An account of value centred on humanity’s epistemic inheritance has little to say to those parents, whose calculation concerns livelihood and dignity rather than knowledge. Revitalisation that ignores this asymmetry preserves a language as data while surrendering it as a means of life.",
