"p": "Sociological common sense once held that the most consequential relationships are the strongest—the kin and close friends whose loyalty can be relied upon. Network analysis complicated this by attending not to the quality of a tie but to its position. Intimates tend to know one another, so the information that reaches a person through any one of them is likely to have reached her through the others; their contributions are redundant. Acquaintances, by contrast, often stand at the edge of other clusters and thus serve as bridges across which novel information travels. The insight is easily misread as a ranking of relationships, as though weak ties were more valuable than strong ones. Its real claim is structural: the value of a tie lies partly in what it connects, not only in what it contains, and the same relationship may be indispensable or superfluous depending on the configuration of the network around it.",
