"p": "The referendum enjoys a reputation as the most direct form of democratic authorisation: the people, unmediated by representatives, pronounce. The reputation depends on treating the question as given. But every referendum is preceded by decisions that determine what the electorate is able to say—which issue is put, how it is worded, whether the alternatives are two or many, what threshold counts as decisive. These choices are made by the very intermediaries the device is supposed to bypass, and they are often more consequential than the vote. A binary ballot, moreover, registers the direction of preference but not its intensity or its conditions, so that a narrow majority indifferent to an outcome may prevail over a minority for whom it is existential. None of this impugns the referendum’s legitimacy as such. It suggests that its claim to directness misdescribes an instrument whose democratic quality is determined largely upstream of the ballot box.",
