"p": "Anthropology’s classic analyses of gift exchange set out to correct a romantic misreading. Where travellers had seen spontaneous generosity, ethnographers found obligation: to give, to receive and to reciprocate, under pain of dishonour. The gift was shown to be interested, agonistic, often a weapon in contests of rank. Yet the correction risked a symmetrical error, collapsing the gift into a slow and euphemised form of market exchange, its apparent disinterest a mask for calculation. What this reduction misses is the temporal structure of the gift. A purchase is consummated at the moment of payment; the parties owe each other nothing further. A gift, because its return is deferred and never exactly equivalent, leaves a residue of obligation that keeps the relationship open. Its interestedness is real, but what it pursues is less a profit than the continuation of a bond, and the market model, which values the clean settlement of accounts, has no way to register that aim.",
