"p": "Histories of tuning often read as a progress narrative: earlier systems, plagued by ‘wolf’ intervals and keys too sour to use, gave way to equal temperament, which distributed the discrepancy evenly and freed composers to modulate at will. The narrative is accurate about the gain and silent about its price. In the older unequal temperaments, keys possessed distinct characters because their intervals genuinely differed, and composers exploited those differences expressively; a passage set in a remote key sounded strained because it was. Equal temperament made every key acoustically identical, so that the expressive associations of keys, which persist in musical rhetoric, now refer to nothing in the sound itself. The transition, in other words, was not from imperfection to perfection but from one bargain to another—trading a palette of differentiated colours for unrestricted mobility. That the second bargain now seems simply natural is a measure of how completely it has reshaped what listeners hear as in tune.",
