"p": "The reputation of postwar concrete architecture has followed a curious trajectory. Reviled for decades as the emblem of a dehumanising modernism, many such buildings are now photographed, catalogued and defended by a generation too young to remember their construction. It would be easy to treat this revival as a correction of earlier philistinism, but that reading assumes the earlier hostility concerned form. Much of it concerned condition: buildings designed for continuous public maintenance were subjected to decades of underinvestment, and the stained concrete and failed services that residents encountered were the visible residue of a withdrawn commitment rather than the intended aesthetic. The revival, conducted largely through images that abstract the buildings from their use, risks repeating the confusion in reverse—admiring as pure form what was conceived as the infrastructure of a social programme. Both hostility and admiration, then, may have judged these buildings as objects while their real fate was decided by policy.",
