"p": "Evaluations of screening programmes face a peculiar epistemic obstacle. Every cancer detected early and treated successfully looks, from the patient’s side and the statistician’s, like a life saved. But increasingly sensitive tests also identify lesions that would never have progressed to symptoms within the patient’s lifetime, and since no one can say which detected lesion belongs to this category, all are treated. The overdiagnosed patient thus bears the full costs of intervention while appearing in the data as a beneficiary, and the more overdiagnosis a programme generates, the better its survival figures look, because harmless lesions are by definition survived. Such distortion is not corrected by gathering more of the same evidence; it is built into the indicator. Only comparisons of mortality between screened and unscreened populations, which are slow and expensive, can disentangle genuine benefit from the illusion produced by counting the indolent among the cured.",
