"p": "Strategy texts have long debated whether entering a market first confers a durable advantage, and the evidence has proved stubbornly mixed. Part of the reason is definitional: studies that count only surviving firms overlook pioneers that failed, inflating the apparent returns to early entry, while studies that define the market narrowly can always find a first mover who succeeded. But the deeper problem is that being first is not a strategy at all. What confers advantage is the presence of mechanisms that convert early entry into protection—switching costs that bind customers, network effects that make a product more valuable as it spreads, scarce inputs that can be pre-empted. Where such mechanisms operate, timing matters a great deal; where they do not, the pioneer merely subsidises the education of its successors. Asking whether it pays to be first is therefore like asking whether it pays to be tall: the answer depends entirely on the game being played.",
