"p": "That widened roads fill with traffic is now well documented, but the phenomenon is often explained misleadingly, as if drivers were irrational or the widening a simple error of forecasting. The better explanation treats road space as an unpriced good. When capacity rises, the time cost of travel falls, and trips that were previously not worth making—at that hour, by that mode, from that distance—become worth making. Traffic expands until the time cost returns roughly to its former level. Nothing here is irrational; each driver responds sensibly to a lower price. The implication is not that new roads are useless, since more people now travel, but that their benefit takes the form of additional trips rather than faster ones. Promising commuters shorter journeys through added capacity thus misdescribes what such investment can deliver, and congestion itself, the symptom the roads were meant to cure, functions as the rationing device that a missing price would otherwise supply.",
