"p": "It is tempting to write the history of zero as the history of a symbol, tracing the mark from its first appearances in inscriptions to its arrival in European arithmetic. Such histories mistake the sign for the concept. Several ancient numeral systems possessed a mark for an empty position, used much as we use the zero in 307, without anyone treating that mark as denoting a quantity. A placeholder is a feature of notation; a number is something that can be operated upon, and admitting ‘nothing’ into the domain of operations raised questions—what is a quantity divided by nothing?—that strained the very notion of quantity. Traditions of formidable mathematical sophistication declined to make the move, not from incapacity but from a defensible conception of number as a multitude of units. The adoption of zero was therefore less a discovery than a revision of what counted as number, and its slowness reflects the depth of that revision rather than any failure of ingenuity.",
