"p": "The argument from determinism to the unreality of free will seems simple: if every choice is the inevitable product of prior causes, no one could have chosen otherwise, and without alternatives there is no freedom. Compatibilists contest not the determinism but the conception of freedom it assumes. What we actually invoke when we distinguish free from unfree acts, they observe, is not the absence of causes but the presence of certain kinds of cause: a theft committed under threat and one committed from greed are equally caused, yet only the second is attributed to the agent without qualification. On this view, freedom is a property of the route by which an action issues from the agent’s own reasons, and its enemies are coercion, compulsion and manipulation rather than causation itself. Critics complain that this changes the subject. Perhaps it does—but if the changed subject is the one our practices of praise and blame were tracking all along, the complaint loses much of its force.",
