"p": "The discovery that remembering is reconstructive—that each recall reassembles an event from fragments, schemas and present cues, and may alter the trace it retrieves—has been received largely as a catalogue of failures. Confident witnesses identify the wrong suspect; vivid recollections of public events prove inconsistent with accounts written the following day. The emphasis is understandable, since these errors carry real costs. But to describe reconstruction as a defect presupposes that memory’s function is archival, a faithful record of the past for its own sake. If its function is instead prospective—to extract what is general from experience and to recombine it in anticipating situations not yet encountered—then the flexibility that permits distortion is the same flexibility that permits generalisation and imagination. A system that stored episodes verbatim would be more accurate and less useful. The errors are not a malfunction of the design but the price of what it was designed to do.",
