"p": "The archive presents itself to the historian as a residue of the past, but it is more accurately a residue of past administration. What survives is what institutions found it useful to record and to keep: assessments, prosecutions, censuses, correspondence among officials. Those outside the circle of power enter such records chiefly at moments of extraction or discipline, and their words, when preserved at all, have passed through the categories and purposes of clerks. A history written from these sources without reflection inherits the archive’s angle of vision and mistakes the priorities of governance for the shape of social life. The remedy is not to abandon the archive, for there is often nothing else, but to read it as an artefact whose omissions are themselves evidence—to ask why a category exists, whose testimony is paraphrased, what the record was meant to accomplish. Silence, read this way, becomes legible: not an absence of history but a trace of the power that decided what history would contain.",
