"p": "Open-source software is often held up as a refutation of the claim that public goods will be underprovided: thousands of volunteers produce code that anyone may use, and the result frequently rivals proprietary products. The refutation is less complete than it appears. What open collaboration solves remarkably well is production—the initial writing of code by people motivated by curiosity, reputation or their own needs. What it solves poorly is maintenance: the unglamorous, indefinite work of patching vulnerabilities, answering bug reports and keeping old components compatible with new ones. Maintenance offers little reputation and serves needs that are mostly other people’s, so it falls disproportionately on a few individuals whose withdrawal can leave critical infrastructure exposed. The public-goods problem, in other words, was not dissolved but displaced, from the creation of the commons to its upkeep, where the classical prediction of underprovision quietly reasserts itself.",
