"p": "It is commonly said that photography liberated painting: relieved of the duty to record appearances, painters were free to pursue colour, surface and subjectivity, and modern art followed. The story is attractive but suspiciously teleological, as though painting had always longed to be abstract and awaited only release. A more exact formulation would say that photography altered the economy of meaning in which any image was received. Once a mechanical process could furnish likeness cheaply, the hand-made image could no longer derive value from likeness alone; whatever it now offered had to be defined by difference from what the camera supplied. Painting’s subsequent course was thus not the unfolding of an essence but a series of negotiations with a rival medium, and the rival was itself transformed in turn, as photographers sought legitimacy by imitating painterly effects. Each medium came to be understood through the other, which suggests that neither possessed the stable identity the liberation story requires.",
