"p": "Debates about translation are often conducted as if the translator’s task were to transfer a meaning from one container to another, and the only question were how much spills. On this model fidelity is the supreme virtue and every departure a loss. But a literary text does not hold its meaning apart from its sounds, rhythms, idioms and allusions; it is constituted by them, and many of them have no counterpart in the receiving language. The translator therefore faces not a single task performed better or worse but a set of incompatible demands—to keep the sense, the register, the music, the strangeness—among which choices must be made. A version that renders every word may lose the tone entirely; one that keeps the tone must take liberties with the words. Fidelity is thus not abandoned but multiplied, and a translation can be judged only by asking to what it chose to be faithful, and whether that choice illuminates the original.",
