"p": "Professional linguists have long regarded popular complaints about ‘incorrect’ usage with amused condescension: languages change, today’s solecism is tomorrow’s standard, and no form is intrinsically better than another. As a description of linguistic structure, this is correct. As an account of what standard languages do, it is incomplete. A codified standard permits communication across regions and generations, stabilises the language of law and administration, and gives learners a target. These are real goods, and they explain why communities whose varieties lack a written standard often seek one. But the same codification creates a scale against which speakers are ranked, and fluency in the prestige variety tracks schooling and class so closely that judgements of correctness become judgements of persons. The scientific neutrality of the linguist and the social anxieties of the purist thus address different objects. The question that matters is not whether a usage is correct, but what work a given standard is doing and for whom.",
