"p": "Plea bargaining is usually defended by necessity: courts could not try every case, and negotiated pleas spare victims the ordeal of testimony while giving defendants a measure of certainty. These benefits are real. What the defence overlooks is the effect of the bargain on the meaning of the alternative it displaces. When the sentence after conviction at trial greatly exceeds the sentence offered for a plea, the decision to plead becomes a calculation of risk in which actual guilt is only one input. A defendant who is innocent but uncertain of acquittal can rationally accept a conviction to avoid a catastrophic one. The trial, nominally the system’s central guarantee, survives chiefly as the threat that makes the offer persuasive, and the larger the gap between the two sentences, the less the outcome reflects evidence at all. The problem is thus not plea bargaining as such but the differential that powers it, which converts a procedure for establishing guilt into a market for pricing risk.",
