"p": "The intuitive model of athletic training is additive: each session deposits a quantum of fitness, and more sessions deposit more. Physiology describes something closer to a dialogue. A training stimulus does not make the body stronger; it damages and depletes it, and performance immediately afterward is worse. Improvement occurs only if the subsequent period of recovery allows the organism to rebuild beyond its previous capacity, in anticipation of further stress. Fitness is thus a response to stress rather than a product of it, and the response requires both the signal and the conditions for answering it. An athlete who intensifies training without proportionate recovery does not accumulate fitness more quickly but accumulates fatigue, sometimes to the point where performance declines for months. On this account rest is not the absence of training but its second half, and the dedication celebrated in sporting culture—never missing a session, always doing more—can be a symptom of misunderstanding what training is.",
