"p": "For two millennia, geometers tried to derive Euclid’s parallel postulate from his other axioms, persuaded that a proposition so evidently true must be a theorem rather than an assumption. When consistent geometries were eventually constructed in which the postulate fails, they were regarded at first as ingenious curiosities—logically impeccable but unrelated to real space. Their significance emerged slowly and was more philosophical than technical. If geometries incompatible with Euclid’s could be as consistent as his, then the axioms of geometry could not be guaranteed by their self-evidence; they were assumptions whose consequences mathematics could explore without deciding whether they held of the world. Which geometry describes physical space became a question for measurement rather than intuition. The episode thus loosened the bond between mathematics and necessary truth about reality, recasting the discipline as the study of possible structures, and in doing so it transformed the meaning of mathematical certainty itself.",
