"p": "Projections of population ageing typically express their alarm through the dependency ratio: the number of people above a fixed age divided by the number of working age. The ratio has the authority of arithmetic, but its categories are conventions. It counts every sixty-five-year-old as dependent and every twenty-five-year-old as productive, though many of the former work, care or volunteer while many of the latter study or seek employment. More importantly, it holds the threshold constant while the thing it was meant to proxy—the onset of frailty—moves. A sixty-five-year-old today is, on most measures of health, closer to a sixty-year-old of a few decades ago. When the age boundary is fixed and health improves, the ratio will mechanically report a deepening crisis even if the actual balance between those who need support and those who provide it changes little. None of this denies that ageing poses fiscal challenges; it suggests that their scale depends heavily on choices about retirement and work that the ratio treats as fixed facts.",
