"p": "The appeal of choice architecture lies in its apparent costlessness. Altering a default, reordering a menu or simplifying a form changes behaviour substantially while forbidding nothing, and so seems to secure better outcomes without the political price of coercion or expenditure. The empirical record confirms that such interventions work—within a domain. They are effective where people already endorse the outcome and fall short of it through inertia, distraction or procrastination. They are far weaker where the obstacle is not attention but constraint: a worker automatically enrolled in a pension may remain at the default contribution because she cannot afford more, and no redesign of the form alters her budget. The risk is therefore not that nudges fail but that their success in the first domain is cited to justify their use in the second, allowing governments to substitute inexpensive adjustments of presentation for the costly redistribution or regulation that constrained choices require.",
