"p": "Epistemology long took the solitary knower as its model: knowledge was belief one could justify for oneself, from one’s own perceptions and inferences, and belief taken on another’s word was knowledge only by courtesy. The model collapses under the slightest inventory of what any of us knows. That the earth orbits the sun, that one’s parents are one’s parents, that a distant city exists—these rest on testimony one has not checked and, in most cases, could not check. Attempts to rescue the model by arguing that we inductively verify the reliability of our informants founder on the same problem: the evidence for reliability is itself largely testimonial. The conclusion is not that we should believe whatever we are told. It is that the epistemic virtue appropriate to creatures like us is not independence but discrimination—the capacity to judge whom to trust, about what, and when—so that dependence becomes a condition of knowledge rather than a falling-away from it.",
