"p": "People consistently overestimate how long and how intensely major events will affect their happiness. Promotions disappoint, setbacks prove survivable, and within a year or two most people report wellbeing close to where it began. From this regularity some writers draw a quietist moral: since we adapt to almost everything, the pursuit of better circumstances is a treadmill, and contentment must be sought within. The inference overlooks the qualifier ‘almost’. Adaptation is markedly uneven. People adjust quickly to larger houses and higher salaries, slowly or not at all to long commutes, chronic noise, unemployment or the loss of close relationships. The pattern suggests that the error is not in valuing circumstances but in misjudging which ones matter: we overweight goods that are conspicuous at the moment of choice and underweight conditions that are experienced continuously. The lesson of adaptation research is therefore not indifference to circumstance but a different and more discriminating attention to it.",
