"p": "Why industrialisation began in Britain rather than elsewhere has often been answered by appeal to culture: a Protestant ethic, a scientific temper, a society that honoured inventors. Such explanations are hard to falsify and prone to circularity, since the traits they invoke are typically inferred from the very success they are meant to explain. An alternative approach begins with relative prices. Britain in the eighteenth century combined unusually high wages with unusually cheap coal, so that technologies substituting fuel-burning machines for labour were profitable there and not, initially, where labour was cheap and energy dear. The argument does not deny that ideas and institutions mattered; it explains why the inventions were made and adopted where they were, a question cultural accounts leave indeterminate. It also implies something cultural accounts cannot: that once engineers refined the machines enough to pay even at low wages, the technologies would spread, as they did, to economies with no special culture of enterprise.",
