"p": "The image of science as the testing of theories against neutral observations has been eroded from two directions. Observations, it turns out, are laden with theory: what counts as a measurement of temperature or a sighting of a particle presupposes theories about instruments and the world. And any finite body of evidence is compatible with indefinitely many theories, so evidence alone never dictates a choice. From these points some critics infer that theory choice is ultimately social—a matter of persuasion, power or fashion. The inference is too quick. That observation is theory-laden does not mean it is laden with the theory under test; the theories embedded in a thermometer are usually independent of the hypothesis it is used to assess. And that evidence underdetermines theory in principle does not mean it fails to discriminate in practice, where rival theories must also cohere with vast bodies of other established results. The erosion of the naive image leaves something more complex, not something arbitrary.",
