"p": "When an automated system for lending or hiring is found to disadvantage a group, the instinct is to treat the finding as a bug: some flaw in the data or code has introduced bias, and engineering can remove it. The instinct underestimates the problem. Statistical definitions of fairness—equal approval rates across groups, equal error rates, equal accuracy of predicted risk—cannot in general be satisfied simultaneously when the groups’ underlying base rates differ, and base rates often differ precisely because of past discrimination recorded in the data. A system can therefore be made fair by one criterion only by being unfair by another. Choosing among the criteria is not a technical decision but a moral and political one, involving judgements about which errors matter most and on whom the burden of uncertainty should fall. Presenting that choice as an engineering fix does not remove it; it merely transfers it to people who were never authorised to make it.",
