"p": "Conservation policy has long sorted species by origin, treating natives as belonging and introduced species as presumptive threats. The presumption rests on real disasters—predators that extinguished island birds, vines that smothered forests—and in such cases eradication is plainly justified. But a criterion based on origin generalises poorly. Most introduced species establish without measurable harm, some now supply food or shelter for natives in landscapes already transformed by agriculture and cities, and the category ‘native’ is itself indexed to an arbitrary date. Meanwhile, the resources devoted to removing harmless newcomers are resources not spent on habitat loss, which threatens far more species. A defensible policy would judge organisms by what they do rather than where they came from, concentrating effort where harm is demonstrated. Critics reply that harm often appears only after a long lag, so that waiting for evidence may mean acting too late—an objection that argues for vigilance and monitoring, not for treating origin as a proxy for damage.",
