"p": "Museums resisting the return of objects acquired under colonial rule typically argue from stewardship and access: the objects are conserved to the highest standard and seen by millions who could not otherwise see them. These considerations are not negligible, and some claimants share them. What is striking is their logical form. Each presents a reason why the objects are well placed where they are, and each is offered by the party currently holding them. When the original transfer was voluntary, such reasons might settle the matter. When it was effected by force or under conditions of domination, the holder’s authority to weigh them is precisely what is in question, and an argument that presupposes that authority cannot be used to establish it. Stewardship and access thus belong in a negotiation between parties with legitimate standing, where they may well influence the outcome. They cannot serve as a unilateral justification, because a justification offered by one party for retaining what it took presupposes the very title it claims to demonstrate.",
