"p": "The novel’s distinctive power, critics have long maintained, is access to other minds: free indirect style and interior monologue let readers inhabit consciousnesses not their own with an intimacy that life seldom permits. From this it is a short step to the claim that reading fiction makes people more empathetic, and the claim has become a staple of arguments for literary education. The first proposition is plausible; the second does not follow from it. Imaginative access to a mind is morally neutral—readers inhabit murderers and manipulators as readily as saints, and the pleasure of doing so need not issue in any change of conduct. Indeed, the very ease of fictional sympathy, exercised without cost, may substitute for the effortful sympathy that actual others require. What the novel cultivates reliably is perhaps a form of attention: the habit of supposing that others have inner lives as dense as one’s own. Whether that habit becomes kindness depends on what readers bring to the book and what they do when they close it.",
