"p": "In many bilingual communities, speakers alternate between languages within a single conversation, even a single sentence, and outsiders—including teachers—often interpret the practice as evidence of deficient command of both. Linguistic research has largely overturned this interpretation. Switches cluster at grammatically predictable points, respecting constraints of both languages at once, and they are deployed for identifiable purposes: to shift footing from joke to rebuke, to mark a quotation, to signal solidarity or distance. Proficient bilinguals switch more, and more intricately, than learners. Far from patching gaps, the practice exploits a resource available only to those who command two systems. This reversal has a pedagogical edge. Where schools treat switching as an error to be corrected, they may be suppressing an advanced competence and teaching children that their most sophisticated linguistic behaviour is a mark of inadequacy—a lesson whose effect on their relationship to both languages is unlikely to be benign.",
