"p": "Theories of secularisation predicted that ritual would wane with religious belief, since ritual was understood as belief enacted. Modern societies have not cooperated. Graduations, national anthems at sporting fixtures, commemorations of the war dead, even the office farewell with its speeches and cake, exhibit the formal properties of rite: prescribed sequence, heightened attention, the marking of a transition, the affirmation of a collectivity. That participants seldom describe these occasions as ritual is not evidence against the description but part of how they function, since a formality announced as sacred would provoke the embarrassment of the secular. The persistence of such practices suggests that the prediction rested on a mistaken order of explanation. Ritual is not primarily the expression of a prior belief; belief may instead be one of the things ritual produces, a gloss upon practices whose work—binding individuals to groups and marking passages in time—proceeds whether or not any doctrine accompanies them.",
