"p": "A common response to populist movements treats their supporters as objects of explanation rather than as reasoning agents: they are said to be misinformed, nostalgic, or manipulated by demagogues. The response has the advantage, for those who offer it, of locating the problem entirely outside the institutions they inhabit. It has the disadvantage of explaining little. Populist appeals gain traction where substantial portions of the electorate conclude, often with evidence, that consequential decisions about trade, migration and fiscal policy were made by elites insulated from their effects and unresponsive to protest through ordinary channels. The populist diagnosis of that grievance may be crude and its remedies dangerous—its hostility to courts, a free press and minorities can corrode the very institutions through which grievances are redressed. But to refuse the diagnosis any truth is to confirm it, for a political class that answers a charge of unresponsiveness by declaring its accusers deluded has demonstrated the accusation.",
