"p": "Classical ethnography adopted a convention of authorial absence: the anthropologist reported what ‘the people’ believed and did, while the circumstances of the reporting—who asked, in what language, with what resources and under whose protection—vanished from the text. Later critics exposed the convention as a rhetoric of authority, and some concluded that ethnography could claim no more than a personal account of encounters. The second conclusion does not follow from the first. That an observer’s position shapes what can be observed is a methodological fact, not an epistemological catastrophe; it is equally true of the astronomer’s telescope and the survey designer’s sampling frame. The appropriate response is the one other sciences adopt: to treat the conditions of observation as part of the data, reporting them so that readers can assess what they enabled and what they foreclosed. An ethnography that discloses its own production is not more subjective than one that conceals it. It is more open to evaluation, and therefore more objective in the only sense the word can usefully bear.",
