"p": "Before recording, a musical performance was an event: it occurred once, before those present, and vanished. Recording converted it into an object that could be repeated indefinitely, and repetition altered the standards by which performance was judged. A slip that would pass unnoticed in a hall becomes, on the tenth hearing, the most conspicuous feature of a record, so recorded performance tended toward a precision and uniformity that earlier performers rarely sought. Audiences then carried those expectations back into the concert hall, measuring live playing against a studio product assembled, often, from many takes. The gains are undeniable—access, preservation, the documentation of interpretive traditions. But the relation between the event and its record has been inverted. The recording, conceived as a trace of performance, has become in many genres its template, and live music is now heard as an approximation of an artefact that no single performance ever produced. The risk-taking that liveness once made meaningful has, correspondingly, lost much of its value.",
