"p": "Photographic images once carried an implicit warrant: whatever appeared before the camera had, in some form, been there, illuminated by real light and registered through a real lens. Even the most elaborate practical effects—models, matte paintings, prosthetics—were physical objects subject to that warrant, and viewers absorbed their consistency with the surrounding world without noticing it. Digital compositing dissolves the warrant. A computer-generated image need not have existed in any form, and its relation to light and gravity is simulated rather than undergone. This does not make it inferior; at its best it is indistinguishable from photography. But it shifts the burden of credibility from the medium to the maker. Where the physical camera imposed limits that guaranteed a baseline coherence, the digital image is limited only by what its makers choose to constrain, and its occasional failures to convince arise less from technical shortfall than from the temptation, licensed by limitless flexibility, to show what no camera could ever have witnessed.",
