"p": "Public campaigns against antibiotic resistance usually address the individual: finish the course, do not demand antibiotics for a cold. The behaviours targeted do contribute, but the framing implies that resistance results from carelessness and can be cured by conscientiousness. Its structure is different. Antibiotic effectiveness is a common resource, depleted by every use, appropriate or not, while the benefits of each use accrue to the user. Farmers who dose livestock to promote growth, pharmacies that sell without prescription where doctors are scarce, and clinicians who prescribe defensively are each acting reasonably given their situations. The incentives also extend to the replenishment of the resource: a new antibiotic, to preserve its efficacy, must be used as little as possible, which makes it a poor commercial prospect, so few are developed. Exhortation to individuals cannot correct a problem in which reasonable choices aggregate into collective harm; only altering the incentives that make those choices reasonable can.",
