"p": "Arguments about the moral status of animals often turn on comparative cognition: whether apes use symbols, whether crows plan, whether fish feel pain in anything like our sense. The research is fascinating, but its moral relevance depends on which moral question is being asked. If the question is whether it is wrong to inflict gratuitous suffering on a creature, the relevant capacity is sentience, not intelligence; pain does not become less bad for being felt by a being that cannot solve puzzles. Tying basic moral consideration to cognitive sophistication would, moreover, have implications for humans of limited cognitive capacity that almost no one accepts. Intelligence may bear on other questions—whether a being has interests in its future, whether killing it painlessly harms it, what forms of life would let it flourish—and on these comparative cognition is genuinely informative. The confusion lies in treating a single ranking of minds as the answer to questions that call on different capacities.",
