"p": "Teachers often return work with both a grade and written comments, on the reasonable assumption that the two together give students more information than either alone. Studies of how students respond suggest otherwise. Faced with a grade, many read it, compare it with their peers’, and pay little attention to the comments; the number answers the question they care most about and closes the transaction. Comments without a grade are more likely to be read and acted on, since they are the only information available. The point is not that grades are worthless—institutions need summary judgements, and students are entitled to know where they stand—but that summary and formative assessment perform different functions and interfere when delivered together. A grade judges a finished piece of work; a comment invites revision. Placing them side by side signals that the work is finished, which is precisely the message that defeats the comment’s purpose.",
