"p": "Critics of shareholder primacy tend to caricature it as an apology for greed, but its most serious defenders make an argument about accountability. A manager instructed to serve all stakeholders—employees, customers, suppliers, communities—can justify almost any decision by invoking one constituency against another, and so escapes evaluation altogether; a single objective, measured by share value, at least permits judgement. The argument is cogent on its own terms. Its weakness lies in two assumptions it requires: that share prices reflect the long-run value of the firm, and that harms imposed on third parties are adequately priced or prohibited by law. Where investors reward quarterly results and regulation lags behind new forms of harm, a manager maximising share value will rationally shorten horizons and externalise costs. The doctrine, then, is not wrong so much as contingent. It disciplines managers only in an institutional environment that it does nothing to secure and that its own pursuit may erode.",
