"p": "Gentrification is usually narrated as displacement: affluent newcomers bid up rents and push out long-standing low-income residents. Displacement does occur, and for those evicted it is a serious harm. Yet studies that follow individual households have repeatedly found that poor residents of gentrifying neighbourhoods move out at rates similar to those of comparable residents elsewhere. Low-income households are mobile everywhere, for reasons of job loss, family change and housing insecurity. What distinguishes gentrifying areas is not the rate of exit but the identity of entrants: departing tenants are replaced by wealthier ones, so the neighbourhood’s composition turns over without unusual expulsion. The finding, if it holds, relocates the problem. The policy question becomes less how to keep current residents in place than how to keep places open to future low-income residents—a question about the overall supply of affordable housing, which gentrification reveals rather than creates.",
