"p": "The arrival of ball-tracking data has transformed professional cricket, and traditionalists lament that calculation is displacing the intuitive craft of captains and bowlers. The lament assumes that data and judgement compete for the same territory. They do not. Statistical models describe regularities across thousands of past deliveries; they are blind to the particular afternoon—the bowler tiring in the heat, the pitch beginning to turn, the batsman whose confidence is visibly ebbing. Judgement, conversely, reads the particular but is prone to the biases of memory and vividness that aggregate data correct. The most effective teams do not choose between the two but arrange their interaction: analysis frames the questions—where is this batsman vulnerable, when has this bowler historically declined—and players answer them in the light of what the moment presents. The real division is not between numbers and instinct but between teams that integrate them and teams that let either govern alone.",
