"p": "The priority dispute over the calculus, with its accusations of plagiarism and its partisan committees, is usually retold as a drama of rival geniuses. The drama is genuine, but the framing of priority distorts what it describes. The two methods were developed largely independently, embodied different conceptions—one grounded in motion and rates, the other in infinitesimal differences and an algebra of symbols—and drew on decades of earlier work in which mathematicians had found tangents and areas for particular curves. What emerged was less a single invention than the convergence of a broad enterprise, at the point where techniques scattered across many problems could be unified. To ask who was first presupposes that there was one thing to be first at, and that ideas are possessions whose ownership can be adjudicated. Both presuppositions owe more to later conceptions of intellectual property than to the way the mathematics actually developed, and the question’s persistence says more about those conceptions than about the history.",
