"p": "The news photograph derives its peculiar authority from a causal connection: light reflected from the scene struck the film or sensor, so that the image is in some sense a trace of what was there. Words describe; photographs, we feel, attest. Yet the trace is the product of a sequence of choices that the image itself conceals—where the photographer stood, which instant was seized, what lay just outside the frame, which of hundreds of exposures an editor selected and with what caption. Each choice can be scrupulous and the result still misleading, for a single image of a single wounded child may convey the reality of suffering while distorting its scale, cause or typicality. None of this licenses the cynical conclusion that photographs lie. It suggests rather that their evidential force and their capacity to mislead have a common source: the trace is real, and its reality lends conviction to a selection that, unlike the trace, is not mechanical at all.",
