"p": "Light pollution has an unusual property among environmental harms: it erases the evidence of its own effects. A person raised under an urban sky, in which a few dozen stars are visible, has no experience of the thousands that a dark sky reveals and so does not register their absence as a loss. Each generation calibrates its sense of the normal night to the sky of its childhood, and as artificial glow spreads, the baseline drifts without anyone perceiving a decline. The ecological effects—disoriented migrating birds, insects drawn fatally to lamps, disrupted hormonal rhythms in many species, including our own—are better documented than the perceptual one, but they too lack a constituency, because the condition they depart from is one few now remember. Unusually, the remedy is cheap and the harm immediately reversible: shielded, dimmer, warmer lighting restores much of the sky at once. The obstacle to remedying light pollution is therefore less technical or economic than perceptual: it is difficult to demand the return of what one has never known was missing.",
