"p": "Gross domestic product attracts criticism from every direction: it counts the cleanup after a disaster as output, ignores unpaid domestic labour and the depletion of natural capital, and says nothing about how income is shared. Each criticism is correct, yet together they are often aimed at a target the statistic was never designed to hit. GDP was constructed to measure the volume of market production, the quantity that matters for gauging recessions, employment and tax capacity, and for that purpose it remains serviceable. The error lies in using it as a proxy for welfare, an error committed by its users rather than built into the measure. The fashionable remedy—a single composite index of wellbeing to replace it—would repeat the mistake in a new form, compressing incommensurable goods into one number whose weights encode contestable value judgements behind a façade of technical neutrality. A dashboard of separate indicators, each measuring what it can, would preserve what GDP does well while refusing to let any single figure stand for the good life.",
