"p": "The contemporary revival of Stoicism presents it as a technology of resilience: distinguish what is in your control from what is not, invest only in the former, and meet misfortune with equanimity. The ancient sources support every element of this advice. What the revival tends to omit is the framework that gave the advice its point. For the Stoics, tranquillity was not the goal but a consequence of virtue, and virtue was inseparable from one’s duties to a community conceived, ultimately, as embracing all rational beings. Detached from that framework, the techniques become ethically indifferent; they can help a person endure an unjust situation as readily as a just one, and may even counsel acceptance of conditions that ought to be resisted. The distinction between what is and is not in one’s control, intended as a spur to act rightly within one’s sphere, can thus be converted into a rationale for withdrawal from public wrongs. A philosophy that once made demands of its practitioners risks becoming a method for accommodating whatever they are given.",
