"p": "Functional imaging studies routinely report that a region ‘lights up’ during some mental activity—moral deliberation, romantic love, political disagreement—and popular accounts then reason backwards, inferring the activity from the activation. The backward inference is valid only if the region is selectively engaged by that activity, and most regions are not. The same area may respond to pain, to error, to uncertainty and to social exclusion, so that observing its activation tells us little about which of these is occurring. The coloured maps, moreover, depict statistical contrasts between conditions rather than images of thought, and their visual vividness lends them a persuasiveness out of proportion to what they show. Imaging has genuinely advanced knowledge of how cognitive functions are distributed in the brain. Its limitation is that location is not explanation: knowing where a difference in blood flow occurs does not tell us what computation is being performed, and that question still requires psychological theory that no scan can supply.",
