"p": "The phrase ‘Silk Road’, coined in the nineteenth century, conjures a highway along which caravans bore silk from China to Rome. Nearly every element of the image misleads. There was no single road but a shifting mesh of routes whose importance waxed and waned with politics and climate; few merchants travelled more than a short segment, so that goods moved through relays of traders each familiar only with neighbouring markets; and silk was one commodity among many, often less significant than horses, paper, metals or slaves. Most importantly, the image of long-distance trade obscures what the network transmitted that was never traded at all: religions, technologies, artistic motifs, crops and pathogens, passing outward in increments too gradual for any participant to notice. If the term is to be retained, its value lies in naming not a commercial artery but a zone of slow, cumulative exchange whose most consequential cargo travelled unpriced and unrecorded.",
