"p": "Biologists have proposed more than twenty definitions of the species, and the proliferation is sometimes cited as evidence that the discipline lacks a coherent foundation. The best-known definition, a group of interbreeding populations reproductively isolated from others, works well for many animals but fails for organisms that reproduce asexually and for plants that hybridise freely; definitions based on shared ancestry or measurable difference resolve some of these cases while generating others. The multiplicity, however, may reflect the subject matter rather than confusion about it. If evolution produces lineages that diverge gradually, acquiring reproductive isolation, distinct ecology and morphological difference at different rates and in different orders, then there will be no single moment at which a population becomes a species, and different criteria will mark different points along the same continuum. On this view, the plurality of definitions is not a scandal awaiting resolution but an accurate record of a process that does not draw sharp lines.",
