"p": "Each wave of mechanisation has provoked predictions of mass unemployment, and each has been followed, eventually, by the emergence of new occupations and a recovery of aggregate employment. The record is often invoked to dismiss present anxieties about automation as a recurring historical error. The reassurance depends on what is meant by ‘eventually’ and by ‘aggregate’. The recoveries took decades and were not self-executing: they depended on workers acquiring new skills, frequently across a generation, and on institutions—mass schooling, labour regulation, social insurance—created through political struggle rather than market adjustment. Many of those displaced never found comparable work; the aggregate recovered while particular lives and regions did not. The historical record thus supports a more conditional conclusion than is usually drawn from it. It shows that economies can absorb technological displacement, not that they will do so automatically or painlessly, and it suggests that the outcome depends on deliberate institutional responses that history’s optimists tend to take for granted.",
