"p": "Large dams have been celebrated as monuments of national progress and condemned as instruments of dispossession, and each verdict can marshal formidable evidence: electricity and irrigation for millions on one side, displaced communities, drowned forests and starved deltas on the other. The persistence of the dispute suggests that the question is badly posed. A dam is not a type of thing with a characteristic balance of costs and benefits; its merits depend on its site—how many people live in the reservoir area, what the river sustains downstream, what alternative sources of power exist. Some dams have repaid their costs many times over; others have destroyed more value than they created. But site-specific assessment has a characteristic blind spot. Costs that fall far from the dam, on fisheries at the river’s mouth or on communities whose silt-fed fields slowly erode, are diffuse, delayed and borne by people outside the project’s accounting. A fair reckoning requires extending the boundary of assessment to include them.",
