"p": "When a celebrated painting is exposed as a forgery, its value collapses though not a brushstroke has changed, and the episode is often cited as proof that the art market prizes signatures rather than art. The inference assumes that what we value in a painting is exhausted by its visible properties. But we also value artworks as achievements, and an achievement is constituted partly by its history: a composition that solved a pictorial problem no one had solved before is a different achievement from a later imitation of that solution, however exact. The forgery may be as pleasing to the eye as the work it imitates; what it cannot be is the invention it simulates. This does not acquit the market of snobbery, much of which is real. It does mean that the drop in value upon exposure is not wholly irrational, since what was lost was not merely a famous name but a genuine property of the object as it had been understood—its status as a first solution rather than a copy of one.",
