"p": "Disputes over the literary canon tend to polarise around two assumptions, each convenient to its holders. Defenders assume that the traditional canon was assembled by disinterested judgements of quality and that revision is driven by politics; revisers assume that judgements of quality were always masks for the interests of those who made them. Neither assumption survives scrutiny. Canons were shaped by who had access to literacy, publication, patronage and the institutions of criticism, which is why women and colonised writers were so long absent from them; to deny this is to mistake the outcome of a restricted competition for the verdict of an open one. Yet the observation that a competition was restricted does not show that its winners lacked merit, and some works have retained their hold on readers across languages, centuries and ideological divides. The coherent position is that a canon should be revised by enlarging the field of comparison, testing inherited works against excluded ones, while continuing to make the judgements of value that canon formation requires.",
