"p": "Alphabetic writing is frequently described as the most advanced script, on the ground that two or three dozen letters suffice to record any utterance, whereas logographic systems demand the memorisation of thousands of characters. The ranking presupposes that the function of writing is to transcribe speech, and judged by that function alone it may be right. But writing serves other ends as well. A script whose characters represent morphemes rather than sounds can be read by speakers of mutually unintelligible varieties, binding a vast and linguistically diverse population to a single written tradition; it also marks distinctions that a phonetic spelling of a language rich in homophones would erase. Skilled readers of such scripts, moreover, recognise characters as rapidly as alphabetic readers recognise words. The cost in learning time is real, but it purchases goods the alphabet cannot provide. The supposed hierarchy of scripts thus reflects not a neutral measure of efficiency but the elevation of one function of writing into the definition of the whole.",
