"p": "Few institutions are as uniformly disparaged as bureaucracy, a word that now connotes delay, rigidity and officials sheltering behind procedure. The disparagement forgets what rule-bound administration was invented to replace. Where officials exercised personal discretion, access to permits, pensions and justice depended on kinship, patronage or bribery, and those without connections had no claim that discretion was bound to honour. Impersonal rules, applied identically to every case, were slow and frequently absurd in particular instances, but they converted favours into entitlements that the unconnected could invoke. The absurdities are the price of the impartiality: a rule flexible enough to avoid every foolish outcome would be flexible enough to accommodate every favoured petitioner. Reforms that promise efficiency by restoring discretion to front-line officials therefore deserve suspicion proportionate to their appeal. The task is to prune procedures that serve no protective function, which requires knowing which rigidities guard against partiality and which merely persist—a distinction that wholesale attacks on bureaucracy make it impossible to draw.",
