"p": "Tourism has long been accused of corrupting the cultures it consumes: rituals are abbreviated to fit itineraries, crafts simplified for the souvenir trade, and living practices converted into performances staged for paying spectators. The charge contains truth, but it rests on a picture of host communities as passive surfaces on which tourist demand inscribes itself. Ethnographic studies frequently find otherwise. Communities distinguish the performances they offer visitors from the practices they keep among themselves, and they use tourist income to sustain traditions that would otherwise have lapsed for want of patrons. Performance for outsiders, moreover, is not inherently inauthentic; representing oneself to others is among the oldest things cultures do. What determines whether tourism hollows out or sustains a tradition is less the presence of spectators than the distribution of control—who decides what is shown, on what terms, and who keeps the proceeds. The corruption thesis, by treating the encounter itself as the harm, directs attention away from these questions of power.",
