"p": "Theatre’s survival in an age of streaming is often explained by nostalgia or subsidy, as if it persisted despite having been superseded. The explanation assumes that theatre and recorded drama compete on the same terms, and on those terms—spectacle, the intimacy of the close-up, convenience—theatre plainly loses. But a performance is not merely a sequence of images and sounds that a recording could capture; it is an event that performers and spectators co-produce. Actors adjust their timing to the house; a silence deepens because the audience holds it; the possibility of failure, present at every moment, lends the moment its tension. A recording preserves the words and gestures but not this reciprocity, which exists only among people present to one another. Theatre endures, then, not because audiences fail to notice that screens do drama better, but because what it offers is not drama in the screen’s sense at all. Measuring it by the standards of recorded media misidentifies what it is.",
