"p": "Heritage protection rests on an intuition few would reject: some buildings and districts embody a value that their owners’ convenience should not be allowed to destroy. Yet the instruments of protection frequently misconstrue what they protect. The streetscapes now listed as historic were themselves products of continual change—houses extended upward and backward, facades refashioned to follow taste, ground floors converted as trades came and went. Their character lay as much in this capacity to absorb alteration as in any particular state they passed through. Regulations that forbid change to a designated moment preserve the appearance while arresting the process that produced it, and by restricting new building they can also drive up costs until the district’s former inhabitants are priced out. The result may be a carefully conserved shell whose life has drained away. Protection aimed at the qualities that make a place valuable—scale, grain, mixture of uses—rather than at the freezing of surfaces might honour the history more faithfully than literal preservation does.",
