"p": "Evidence-based medicine displaced a culture in which treatments were justified by authority, habit and the vivid memory of striking cases, and the displacement eliminated many useless and harmful practices. Its critics do not propose a return to that culture. Their objection concerns the gap between what trials establish and what clinicians must decide. A randomised trial estimates an average effect in a population selected for tractability, frequently excluding the elderly, the pregnant and those with several conditions—the very patients who crowd general practice. Applying that average to a particular person requires judging how she differs from the trial’s participants and how those differences bear on benefit and harm, and no trial can make that judgement for the clinician. When guidelines derived from trials are treated as rules rather than as evidence to be weighed, the movement’s founding insight is inverted: authority returns, now in the form of the protocol, and the individual patient disappears behind the average to which she has been assigned.",
