"p": "Conventional criminal procedure construes crime as an offence against the state: the state prosecutes, the state punishes, and the person harmed appears chiefly as a witness to a wrong that has been, in a sense, appropriated. Restorative approaches reverse the construal, treating crime as a breach in relations between particular people and inviting offenders to meet those they harmed, hear the consequences and take part in repair. Reported outcomes are encouraging—many victims prefer such meetings to trials, and some evaluations find lower reoffending—but the approach carries risks that its advocates sometimes understate. Encounters can retraumatise victims of violence; power imbalances between parties can be reproduced in the room; offenders may perform contrition to obtain leniency. These hazards do not discredit the approach. They indicate that its value is conditional on voluntary participation and careful mediation, and that its distinctive contribution is to restore to victims a role in their own case that conventional procedure has largely taken from them.",
