"p": "The argument that those with nothing to hide have nothing to fear from surveillance treats privacy as a shield for wrongdoing, valuable only to those with something discreditable to conceal. The argument fails even on its own terms, since what counts as discreditable depends on who is watching and on rules that can change. But its deeper error is to locate the harm of surveillance in disclosure. Much of the harm occurs before anything is disclosed, in the adjustments people make because they know they may be observed. Aware of being watched, people avoid not only illegal acts but unpopular associations, unconventional reading, tentative opinions voiced before they are fully formed—the experiments through which individuals and societies discover what they think. The cost is therefore borne by the innocent as much as the guilty, and it is invisible, since it consists of things that were never done. A society that measures surveillance’s harms by the secrets it exposes will systematically miss its principal effect, which is the narrowing of what people dare to try.",
