"p": "Brands are often dismissed as instruments of manipulation, devices for charging a premium on goods no better than their generic equivalents. In many markets the charge is accurate. It nonetheless overlooks an economic function that brands perform where buyers cannot judge quality before purchase. A medicine, a car part or a packaged food conceals its quality until it is used, and sometimes long after. In such conditions a brand works as a bond posted by the seller: having invested heavily in a reputation that a single scandal could destroy, the firm has a stake in maintaining quality that an anonymous producer lacks, and the buyer can rely on that stake without inspecting the product. The function is strongest precisely where quality is hardest to verify and weakest where goods are simple and easily compared. It follows that the brand premiums that look most like pure manipulation—on salt, sugar or bottled water—are also the ones least defensible on informational grounds, while premiums elsewhere may be partly earned.",
