"p": "The puzzle of the ship whose planks are replaced one by one is usually treated as a question about what the ship really is. [1] On this view, once every fact about the planks, their arrangement, and their history has been specified, nothing further remains to be settled; to ask whether it is ‘still the same ship’ is to request a ruling, not to seek a discovery. [2] Applied to ships, the thought seems harmless, even liberating, since nothing of consequence hangs on how a harbour authority chooses to keep its registers. [3] Applied to persons, it is disquieting, because we ordinarily assume that whether some future individual will be me is a matter of deep fact, one on which my prudential concern rightly depends. [4] If identity is merely a convenient way of summarising continuities of memory, character, and body, then that concern may be resting on less than it supposes. Some philosophers have found this conclusion consoling rather than threatening, since it loosens the grip of anxieties about death that presuppose a further, all-or-nothing fact.",
