"p": "Economic historians long treated the medieval craft guild as a textbook cartel, an association whose rules on prices, entry, and output served its members at the expense of consumers and excluded talented outsiders. [1] The indictment is not mistaken so much as incomplete, for it judges the guild by the standards of a market in goods while ignoring its role in a market that scarcely existed without it. [2] By binding the apprentice for a fixed term and controlling his eventual admission to mastership, the guild made such training enforceable, solving a problem of contract that would otherwise have left valuable skills untaught. [3] On this reading, the restrictions that look like cartel behaviour were in part the price of a functioning system of vocational training. [4] Sceptics reply that the same rules could serve both purposes at once, and that masters routinely used apprenticeship terms to favour their own sons and limit competition. The guild, in other words, may have been simultaneously a school and a cartel, and the historical question is which function dominated in which trades.",
