"p": "Histories of the shipping container tend to celebrate the box itself, a steel crate of standard dimensions that could pass from lorry to ship to train without being unpacked. [1] Yet the box was technically trivial; similar crates had been tried for decades without transforming anything. [2] What changed in the 1960s was the slow, contested agreement among shipping lines, port authorities, unions, and manufacturers of cranes and chassis to build around a single set of dimensions and corner fittings. [3] Until that agreement existed, each early adopter bore the full cost of specialised equipment while capturing only a fraction of the savings, since every transfer to an incompatible system reintroduced manual handling. [4] Increasing returns of this sort make standards notoriously hard to establish and, once established, notoriously hard to displace, which is why the dimensions fixed in that decade still govern a fleet built half a century later. The container’s real lesson may be that the decisive innovations are often institutional, lying less in a device than in the conventions that make devices interchangeable.",
