"p": "Renaissance painting before 1600 generally distributed light with an even hand, so that a picture could be read like a well-composed sentence in which every clause receives its due. [1] Caravaggio’s innovation was not simply to deepen the shadows but to make light selective, falling on a hand, a face, or a coin while leaving most of the canvas in near-darkness. [2] Contemporaries were quick to give that verdict a theological sense: in a picture of a calling or a conversion, the beam that singles out one figure enacts the very grace the scene depicts. [3] Light ceases to be the medium in which the story is told and becomes one of its agents. [4] The manner spread so quickly through Europe that within a generation it had hardened into formula, its dramatic contrasts applied indiscriminately to subjects that gave them no meaning. Only a few painters, Rembrandt chief among them, recovered the sense that darkness and light in a picture might carry an argument rather than merely produce an effect.",
