"p": "The intervals that Western ears hear as most consonant correspond to simple frequency ratios—two to one for the octave, three to two for the fifth. [1] It is a stubborn fact of arithmetic, however, that no power of three-halves equals a power of two, so a cycle of pure fifths never returns exactly to its starting pitch. [2] Every tuning system for fixed-pitch instruments is therefore a policy for distributing an unavoidable error. [3] Equal temperament, by contrast, spreads the error uniformly, purchasing complete freedom of modulation at the cost of making every interval except the octave slightly impure. [4] The trade was so successful that its costs became inaudible to most modern listeners, who have grown up with no other standard and cannot miss what they have never heard. Yet some historians of performance argue that pieces written for unequal temperaments lose a dimension in equal tuning, since the distinct colour each key once possessed was part of what a composer meant by choosing it.",
"sentence": "Earlier temperaments concentrated it in a few remote keys, which became unusable but left the commonly played ones sweeter than any equal division could allow.",
