"p": "A masonry vault does not merely press down on its supports; it also pushes outward, and the wall that receives it must resist both forces at once. [1] The Gothic solution was to separate the two functions that the older method had fused, carrying the outward thrust through flying buttresses to piers set away from the building while leaving the wall free to enclose space rather than to carry the roof. [2] Freed from structural duty, the wall could dissolve into glass, and interiors could be flooded with coloured light. [3] Nineteenth-century theorists saw in this an early instance of rational construction, in which every visible element expresses a structural necessity and nothing is mere ornament. [4] Later engineering analysis has complicated that reading, showing that some ribs and pinnacles do little structural work and that medieval builders often reached their forms by rule of thumb and precedent rather than calculation. The cathedral looks like a diagram of forces partly because we have learned, from those nineteenth-century theorists, to see it as one.",
"sentence": "For centuries builders answered this with sheer mass, thickening walls until they could contain the thrust within themselves, at the price of keeping openings small and interiors dim.",
