"p": "Clinical trials were designed to treat the placebo response as noise, a quantity to be subtracted so that a drug’s specific pharmacological effect could be measured cleanly against it. [1] That design embodied an assumption, rarely stated because it seemed obvious: that the response depends on deception, on the patient’s false belief that an inert pill is a medicine. [2] If the response survives disclosure, its source must lie less in what the patient believes about the pill than in the ritual of treatment itself—the consultation, the prescription, the act of taking something on schedule. [3] Some researchers prefer the term ‘meaning response’ for this reason, since what heals appears to be the significance of the encounter rather than any property of the substance. [4] The ethical implications of this shift cut in two directions. Disclosure removes the objection that prescribing placebos requires lying, yet the finding also suggests that much of what medicine attributes to its drugs may belong, in part, to its manner of delivering them.",
