"p": "Two drivers, equally inattentive, glance at their phones on the same road; one returns his eyes to an empty street, while the other looks up to find that he has killed a child. [1] Yet what grounds so stark a difference in judgement lies wholly outside their control, in the chance presence of a child at a particular moment. [2] If responsibility tracks only what an agent controls, the divergence in our verdicts is a mistake, and the two drivers deserve identical blame. [3] The difficulty is that the principle of control, applied consistently, does not stop at outcomes; character, circumstance, and even the options one happens to face are equally unchosen, shaped by upbringing, temperament, and the accidents of history. [4] Pressed far enough, the principle leaves almost nothing for which anyone could be blamed or praised. The problem of moral luck is therefore not a curiosity about car accidents but a challenge to the coherence of our ordinary practice of holding one another responsible.",
