"p": "The rationale for the altitude camps favoured by endurance athletes is physiological and, in outline, uncontroversial. [1] Reduced oxygen pressure stimulates the kidneys to release erythropoietin, which in turn raises the mass of oxygen-carrying red cells, and the gain persists for some weeks after descent. [2] The same hypoxia that drives this adaptation, however, prevents athletes from sustaining the training intensities they could achieve at sea level, so that the haematological gain may be bought with a loss of conditioning. [3] Controlled trials of the approach have nonetheless produced inconsistent results, and the inconsistency appears to lie less in the method than in the athletes. [4] Some individuals show large increases in red-cell mass after a few weeks of exposure; others, apparently healthy and well trained, show almost none. If the difference is substantially inherited, as twin and family studies suggest, then the question is not whether altitude training works but for whom, and a uniform prescription for a whole squad is the wrong answer to it.",
