"p": "The competitive model of the labour market predicts that a minimum wage set above the market rate must reduce employment, since firms will hire fewer workers once labour costs more. [1] The prediction depends on an assumption easily overlooked: that each employer is too small to influence the wage and must pay whatever the market dictates or lose its staff entirely. [2] Where workers face few alternative employers within reach, or bear high costs in searching and switching, that assumption fails. [3] Underpaying in this way comes at a cost to the firm itself, however, since attracting one more worker requires raising the wage for everyone already employed, and so it hires fewer people than a competitive firm would. [4] A minimum wage set within the right range removes that disincentive, and can therefore raise employment rather than lower it. The empirical literature comparing adjacent regions with different minimum wages has found disemployment effects much smaller than the competitive model predicts, which is at least consistent with widespread wage-setting power.",
