"p": "Wegener’s hypothesis of continental drift, proposed in 1912, is often cited as a case of a correct idea rejected by a dogmatic establishment. [1] The evidence he assembled was considerable: the fit of the Atlantic coastlines, matching fossils on continents now separated by oceans, and geological formations that seemed to continue across them. [2] What he could not supply was a mechanism, and the forces he proposed were shown by physicists to be many orders of magnitude too weak to plough continents through the ocean floor. [3] When the mechanism arrived in the 1960s, in the form of sea-floor spreading, it came from an unexpected direction and showed that Wegener had been right about the motion but wrong about how it occurred. [4] The continents do not plough through the ocean floor; they ride upon plates whose margins are continually created and consumed. The episode illustrates less the triumph of a lonely genius than the reasonable demand that correlations be backed by causes before they are admitted into science.",
"sentence": "The sceptics were therefore not refusing to look at evidence; they were declining to accept a pattern that, as far as anyone then knew, no physical process could have produced.",
