"p": "Public-key cryptography is often said to have solved the problem of key distribution, allowing strangers to communicate securely without first meeting to exchange a secret. [1] Hence the danger: if an adversary can substitute his own key for that of the intended recipient, the mathematics will protect the conversation perfectly while delivering it to the wrong person. [2] The problem of distributing secrets has thus been transformed into a problem of authenticating keys, and that problem is not mathematical but irreducibly institutional and social. [3] Certificate authorities answer it by vouching for the binding between a key and an identity, which relocates trust rather than eliminating it. [4] Several such authorities have been compromised or have issued certificates carelessly, and each failure has revealed how much of the system’s security depends on organisations that most users have never heard of. Alternative schemes distribute the vouching among users themselves, but they have struggled to achieve adoption beyond specialist communities. The mathematics, in short, is the easy part; the persistent difficulty is knowing whose key one is holding.",
"sentence": "The claim is true of secrecy but incomplete as to trust, because encrypting a message to a published key secures it only for whoever actually controls that key.",
"answer": 1,
"why": "The sentence qualifies the claim in the first sentence—secrecy, not trust, has been secured—and its point that a key protects only whoever controls it is what the next sentence’s ‘Hence the danger’ builds on. [2] is tempting because ‘The claim’ can reach back to the opening and the substituted-key case illustrates the qualification, but if the sentence is moved there, ‘Hence the danger’ after [1] follows only the claim that the problem was solved, where no danger has been raised."
