"p": "Ecologists have long sought traits that predict which introduced species will become invasive, and the search has been largely disappointing. [1] One influential hypothesis attributes invasiveness not to any trait of the invader but to an absence: the specialised predators, parasites, and pathogens left behind in its native range. [2] Released from these enemies, the newcomer can redirect resources from defence to growth and reproduction, outcompeting natives that remain burdened. [3] The hypothesis has real support, yet it struggles with a puzzling feature of many invasions—a lag of decades between arrival and explosive spread. [4] Some researchers explain the lag by the slow accumulation of genetic variation through repeated introductions; others by the chance arrival of a mutualist, such as a pollinator, that the species had lacked. Invasiveness, on these accounts, is less a property of a species than of a particular encounter between a species and a place. The same plant may languish for a century in one region and overrun another within a decade, for reasons that have little to do with the plant alone.",
"sentence": "If enemy release were the whole story, the advantage should be greatest on arrival and diminish as local enemies adapt to the newcomer, not the other way round.",
