"p": "The arrival of photography in the 1830s is often said to have freed painting from the obligation to record appearances. [1] The formulation is attractive, since the century that followed saw painting move steadily away from illusionistic description toward colour, surface, and subjective perception. [2] Altarpieces, allegories, and history paintings had never aimed merely to record, and portrait painters had always flattered, idealised, and composed rather than transcribed. [3] What photography displaced was a narrower range of practices—the miniature, the topographical view, the cheap likeness—whose markets collapsed within a generation as studio photographers undercut them on price. [4] The broader shift in painting may owe as much to new pigments, new patrons, and the institution of the independent exhibition as to competition from the camera. The liberation thesis, in short, takes a real effect in a few genres and extends it to an art that had never been as bound to description as the thesis requires. Its persistence owes more to the appeal of a tidy narrative than to the history of painting.",
"sentence": "It implies, however, that painters had previously regarded description as their chief task, which is true of some genres and doubtful for most others.",
