"p": "Novelists before the nineteenth century generally kept a clear boundary between a narrator’s voice and a character’s thoughts, marking the latter with quotation marks or with verbs such as ‘she thought’ or ‘he wondered’. [1] Free indirect discourse dissolves that boundary, rendering a character’s thoughts in the third person and past tense of narration while retaining the character’s own idiom, emphases, and evaluations. [2] A sentence such as ‘Tomorrow she would leave this dreadful town for ever’ belongs grammatically to the narrator and emotionally to the character. [3] Irony thrives on that indeterminacy, since a narrator can reproduce a character’s self-deceptions without endorsing them and without stepping forward to correct them. [4] Critics have also seen in the technique a means of representing consciousness from inside without abandoning the authority of a narrating intelligence. It thus occupies a distinctive position between the omniscient narrator of the earlier novel and the stream-of-consciousness experiments of the twentieth century. Its effects depend on a reader alert enough to hear two voices in a single sentence.",
"sentence": "Few sentences divide so neatly, however; more often the reader cannot tell where one voice ends and the other begins, and the uncertainty is the point rather than a defect of the method.",
"answer": 3,
