"p": "Creole languages arise when children acquire as their first language a pidgin, the reduced contact language improvised by adults who share no common tongue. [1] The striking fact, for some linguists, is that these additions resemble one another across creoles that formed independently, on different continents and from different source languages. [2] They have taken the resemblance as evidence of an innate grammatical template, a default structure that children impose when their input underdetermines a grammar. [3] Critics reply that the similarities are overstated, and that what is shared can often be traced to features of the languages spoken by the adults who formed the pidgin in the first place. [4] The debate matters beyond creole studies, since creoles offer something close to a natural experiment in how grammar emerges. If children reliably supply structure their input lacks, the case for a biological contribution to language becomes considerably harder to resist. If they do not, creoles become instead a record of contact, preserving the traces of the languages that met on the plantation and in the port.",
