"p": "Public debate about gentrification centres on displacement, the forced departure of low-income residents as rents and property taxes rise around them and familiar shops give way to newer, costlier ones. [1] Several careful studies have found, to the surprise of their authors, that poorer households in gentrifying neighbourhoods move no more often than comparable households elsewhere. [2] The finding has been seized upon by those who regard gentrification as largely benign, since it seems to show that the feared exodus does not occur. [3] The inference is too quick, because a neighbourhood can change its composition without anyone being forced out. [4] Researchers call this exclusionary displacement, and it is invisible in studies that track only whether existing residents leave. It also means that the costs of gentrification fall largely on people who never lived in the area, and who therefore rarely appear in accounts of the neighbourhood’s transformation. A measure that counts only the departures of current residents will therefore systematically understate a process whose main victims are the people who are never able to arrive.",
