"p": "A referendum appears to be the purest expression of popular sovereignty, a question put directly to the people and settled by their answer. [1] Its simplicity, however, conceals a problem of aggregation, because the ballot must reduce a multidimensional disagreement to a binary choice. [2] Once the vote is won, those camps must agree on which change to make, and whichever version prevails will be opposed by many who voted for change in the first place. [3] The result can be a decision that commands majority support in the abstract and minority support in every concrete form. [4] Some constitutions mitigate the problem by requiring that the details of a proposal be fixed before the vote, or by holding a second referendum once negotiation has given the change a definite shape. Both devices sacrifice some of the referendum’s appealing simplicity, which suggests that the simplicity was part of the problem all along. A question that can be answered in a word may be a question that should not have been asked in that form.",
