"p": "English kinship terminology collapses distinctions that many other systems preserve, using ‘uncle’ for a father’s brother and a mother’s brother alike. [1] To English speakers the usage seems simply to name a biological relation, as though the category existed in nature and English had merely found a word for it. [2] Systems that distinguish the two uncles, however, typically do so because the two men stand in different social positions to the child, with different rights over him and different duties toward him. [3] To translate both terms as ‘uncle’ is therefore to impose the English map of obligations on a territory it does not fit. [4] Early ethnographers made exactly this mistake, reading terminologies as clumsy attempts at genealogy rather than as precise descriptions of social roles. The lesson extends well beyond kinship: the categories of one’s own language feel natural precisely because they have never been tested against a system that carves the world differently. Translation, at its best, is the occasion on which such testing begins in earnest.",
