"p": "The romantic image of jazz improvisation as creation from nothing does not survive close listening to the recorded solos of its masters. [1] On the strength of such inventories, some critics have concluded that improvisation is largely a matter of recombining memorised formulas, much as oral epic poets were shown to assemble their lines from stock phrases fitted to the metre. [2] The comparison is illuminating but can mislead, because the significance of a formula lies in how it is placed. [3] A familiar figure displaced by half a beat, set against an unexpected harmony, or broken off at a moment of tension becomes a different musical statement, as a quotation changes meaning with its context. [4] The improviser’s originality therefore resides less in the invention of material than in the judgement exercised over its use in real time. That judgement is itself learned, through years of listening and imitation, which is why the tradition insists that one must master the vocabulary before one can be said to speak. Fluency, in music as in speech, is the condition of saying something new.",
"sentence": "Transcription reveals a repertoire of recurring figures—phrases, rhythmic cells, ways of approaching a chord—that a player deploys again and again across different tunes and decades.",
