"p": "The architects of brutalism justified their exposed concrete in moral terms, as an honesty about materials and construction that decorative cladding concealed. [1] The aesthetic was bound up with a social programme, since many of the buildings were universities, civic centres, and housing estates commissioned by an expanding welfare state. [2] That association proved fateful, for when the welfare state retrenched, the buildings were among the first casualties of reduced maintenance. [3] Concrete that had been designed to weather nobly instead streaked and spalled, and estates that had been planned as communities became bywords for neglect. [4] The recent revival of affection for brutalism, among critics and on social media, may be read partly as a correction of that verdict, judging the buildings by their forms rather than by their histories of neglect. Whether the revival will extend to the estates themselves, as opposed to photographs of them, is another matter. Photographs flatter concrete in ways that wet winters and broken lifts do not, and the people who live in the buildings may judge them very differently from those who admire them online.",
