"p": "Antibiotic resistance is often described as a medical problem, but its underlying structure is that of a commons, like a fishery or a pasture. [1] The effectiveness of an antibiotic is a shared resource, depleted a little by every use, since each course of treatment selects for whatever resistant strains happen to be present. [2] Made one patient at a time on such terms, individually reasonable decisions predictably sum to overuse. [3] The commons framing also explains the failure of the market to supply new antibiotics, because a novel drug that ought to be held in reserve to preserve its effectiveness will earn its maker little revenue. [4] Proposals to address this include subscription models, in which governments pay a fixed fee for access to a new antibiotic regardless of the volume used. Such schemes decouple a company’s reward from its sales, aligning commercial incentives with the stewardship that the commons requires. Whether governments will pay for drugs they hope never to use widely is, however, a test of political patience as much as of economic design.",
