"p": "Debates over trial by jury usually turn on competence, on whether untrained citizens can evaluate complex evidence as reliably as professional judges who have spent careers weighing it. [1] Studies that ask judges to record the verdict they would have reached in cases they have just tried find that judge and jury agree in the large majority of cases. [2] Where they disagree, the disagreement is not random: juries acquit more often than judges would. [3] The asymmetry suggests instead that juries apply a more demanding standard of proof, or weigh the consequences of conviction more heavily, than professionals who have heard many similar cases. [4] Whether this is a defect depends on one’s view of the criminal trial. For those who hold that wrongful conviction is a far graver error than wrongful acquittal, a decision-maker who leans toward acquittal is doing exactly what the system was designed to ensure. For those who give more weight to convicting the guilty, the same pattern looks like a systematic bias, and no study of competence can settle which view is correct.",
