"p": "The ethics of whistleblowing is usually framed as a conflict between loyalty to an organisation and duty to the public. [1] The framing concedes too much to the organisation, since it treats loyalty as owed to the institution as such rather than to the purposes that justify its existence. [2] A hospital exists to heal, a bank to safeguard deposits, a regulator to protect the public from the harms it is charged with preventing. [3] An employee who exposes an organisation’s betrayal of its own purpose may therefore be the most loyal member it has, loyal to what it is supposed to be rather than to what its managers have made of it. [4] Such assessments are fallible, and organisations predictably exploit their fallibility, portraying disclosures as the acts of disgruntled or confused employees. The case for legal protection rests partly on this asymmetry, since an individual bearing the risk of misjudgement faces an institution that bears almost none. Protection does not make the judgement easier, but it lowers the price of getting it wrong in good faith.",
