"p": "When test scores become the basis for rewarding or sanctioning schools, the scores tend to rise, often quickly and sometimes dramatically. [1] The standard way of telling the two apart compares gains on the high-stakes test with performance on independent assessments of the same subject, which no one has an incentive to prepare for. [2] In many systems the gains shrink sharply or vanish on the independent measures, suggesting that what improved was familiarity with a particular instrument. [3] Teachers under pressure are not necessarily cynical; they narrow instruction toward what is tested because the incentives tell them, accurately, that this is what will be counted. [4] The general principle, articulated by social scientists in several fields, is that a quantitative indicator used for consequential decisions becomes subject to pressures that corrupt the very process it was meant to monitor. The remedy is not to abandon measurement but to measure in ways that are costly to game, and to lower the stakes attached to any single number. Scores that matter less are, paradoxically, scores that can be believed more.",
