"p": "Franchising is commonly explained as a way for a growing chain to raise capital, since franchisees finance their own outlets out of their savings. [1] The explanation is incomplete, because a chain could often borrow more cheaply than its franchisees and would then capture the profits of every outlet itself. [2] That monitoring problem grows with distance, since an outlet far from headquarters is costly to inspect and its results are hard to attribute. [3] Consistent with this, chains tend to own their outlets near headquarters and to franchise those in remote locations. [4] The arrangement creates its own frictions, however, since an owner maximising his outlet’s profit may skimp on quality in ways that damage the brand shared by all. Much of a franchise contract can be read as an attempt to manage this tension, granting owners the residual profit while constraining the choices through which they might exploit it. The contract, in effect, rents out the brand while trying to keep its reputation from being spent by the tenants.",
