"p": "Suburban layouts of loops and cul-de-sacs were designed to protect residential streets from through-traffic, and in that narrow aim they largely succeeded. [1] Their effect on movement as a whole was less benign, because a hierarchical network funnels every trip, however short, onto a few arterial roads. [2] Two houses whose gardens back onto one another may be separated by a journey of a kilometre or more, and a shop visible from a bedroom window may be unreachable on foot without a long detour. [3] Researchers measuring such networks distinguish distance as the crow flies from distance along the network, and the ratio between the two is consistently higher in cul-de-sac suburbs than in older grids. [4] Retrofitting usually involves cutting pedestrian paths between the heads of adjacent cul-de-sacs, restoring permeability for walkers while still excluding cars. Such schemes often meet resistance from residents who value the seclusion of their streets, which shows that the original design delivered something people genuinely wanted, even as it imposed costs on everyone else.",
