"p": "Training does not make an athlete fitter; recovery from training does. [1] A demanding session leaves the body temporarily weaker, its muscle fibres damaged and its energy stores depleted, and it is during the subsequent rest that the body rebuilds to a level slightly above its previous capacity. [2] The model implies that the timing of the next stimulus matters as much as its size, since a session that arrives before rebuilding is complete stacks fresh damage onto unrepaired tissue. [3] The resulting syndrome, marked by persistent fatigue, disturbed sleep, and depressed mood as well as poor performance, can take months to resolve. [4] Its insidiousness lies in the fact that its early symptoms resemble the ordinary tiredness of hard training, which athletes are taught to push through. Many programmes now monitor heart-rate variability and mood questionnaires for warning signs, on the reasoning that by the time performance falls, the damage has already been done. The lesson that rest is part of training is easy to state and, for driven athletes, remarkably hard to accept.",
