"p": "The norm of journalistic objectivity is usually presented as a professional ethic, a commitment to facts over opinion that reporters adopted as their craft matured. [1] Sold for a penny, such a sheet could not survive on subscriptions from a single party or faction; it needed readers of every persuasion and the advertisers who wished to reach them all. [2] Partisanship, which had sustained the older press, became a commercial liability, since every reader alienated was revenue forgone. [3] News presented as neutral fact rather than argument could be sold to the whole market at once. [4] None of this makes objectivity a sham; norms born of interest can come to be held sincerely and to discipline those who hold them. But it suggests that the norm may be vulnerable when its economic basis erodes, as it has in a media environment that rewards the cultivation of loyal partisan audiences over the pursuit of a general one. Objectivity, on this account, is a historical achievement that depends on conditions, not a timeless virtue that maintains itself.",
