"p": "A durable myth holds that medieval Europeans prized spices because they disguised the taste of meat on the verge of spoiling. [1] The explanation founders on simple economics, since the households that could afford pepper and cloves were precisely those that could afford fresh meat, and spices cost far more than the food they supposedly rescued. [2] Medieval cookery books, moreover, prescribe spices for fish, sweets, and wine as liberally as for meat, which is hard to reconcile with a purely corrective function. [3] To serve them lavishly was therefore to display not only wealth but learning and refinement, a command of goods and ideas that few could share. [4] Such an account also explains the abrupt change in elite taste in the seventeenth century, when Dutch and English shipping made spices plentiful and cheap. Once anyone could afford them, they ceased to distinguish anyone, and fashionable French cooking turned instead to butter, herbs, and the flavours of the ingredients themselves. The history of spices is thus less a chapter in the history of preservation than in the history of distinction.",
