"p": "River deltas look like permanent features of the map, but their persistence is the outcome of a contest rather than a fact of geography. [1] For most of the last ten thousand years supply outran loss, and the great deltas of Asia, Africa, and the Americas grew into some of the most fertile and densely populated land on earth. [2] Over the past century the advantage has passed to the other side. [3] Dams upstream now trap a large share of the sediment that once reached the coast, levees confine what remains to channels that discharge it offshore, and the pumping of groundwater accelerates the sinking of the land. [4] Many deltas are consequently subsiding several times faster than the sea is rising, so that their inhabitants face a relative rise in sea level far greater than global averages suggest. The usual framing of coastal risk in terms of climate change alone therefore misdescribes the situation in the places most exposed to it. Some engineers now propose deliberately breaching levees to let rivers spread sediment again, accepting periodic flooding as the price of keeping the land above the sea.",
