"p": "For several decades endurance athletes were advised to drink enough during exercise to replace all the fluid they lost in sweat, and ideally to drink before they felt thirsty. [1] Neither assumption survived scrutiny. [2] Studies of marathon runners found that the fastest finishers often lost the most weight, and that the most dangerous condition among slower runners was not dehydration but hyponatraemia, a dilution of blood sodium caused by drinking too much. [3] Several deaths from this condition prompted a revision of guidelines, which now generally recommend drinking according to thirst. [4] Historians of sports science have traced how these beliefs became orthodoxy, noting that the earlier advice was promoted vigorously by the makers of sports drinks, who funded much of the research on which it rested. The episode illustrates how a commercially convenient hypothesis can harden into received wisdom, not through fraud but through the cumulative weight of studies designed and publicised by interested parties. Scepticism about who funds a study is not an accusation of dishonesty but a recognition of how research questions come to be framed.",
