"p": "The mathematical theory of probability is often dated to an exchange of letters in 1654 concerning a gambler’s problem: how to divide the stakes fairly when a game of chance is interrupted before either player has won. [1] Earlier writers had proposed dividing the pot in proportion to the rounds each player had already won, a solution that seems natural enough. [2] The correspondents saw that the stakes should be divided according to each player’s chance of winning had the game continued, a quantity that could be calculated by enumerating the possible outcomes of the remaining rounds. [3] The shift seems small, but it introduced a new kind of object into mathematics: the expectation, a value assigned to an uncertain future. [4] Within a century the same idea underwrote the pricing of annuities and insurance, enterprises that consist entirely in placing present values on future contingencies. It is a curious fact that a theory now indispensable to physics, finance, and medicine began as a question of fairness between two gamblers at an unfinished table.",
