"p": "In the seventeenth century tea and sugar were luxuries in Britain, consumed by the wealthy as markers of refinement and taste. [1] By the early nineteenth century both had become staples of the working-class diet, consumed together several times a day in households that could afford little else. [2] Contemporary moralists were puzzled and often indignant, complaining that the poor were squandering scarce wages on a beverage without nourishment. [3] Their complaint overlooked what the combination supplied: sweetened tea delivered calories, warmth, and a mild stimulant at a cost far below that of the food or fuel that might otherwise have provided them. [4] Some historians go further, arguing that the cheap calories of colonial sugar helped make possible the intensified labour regimes of industrial Britain. On this reading, the cup of tea on a factory worker’s table linked the plantations of the Caribbean to the mills of Lancashire, and the pleasures of consumption to the disciplines of production. A habit once read as a mark of improvidence thus appears, in retrospect, as an adaptation to the new demands of industrial work.",
