"p": "No flat map can represent the surface of a sphere without distortion, a fact that follows from geometry rather than from any deficiency of cartographic skill. [1] Every projection therefore chooses which properties to preserve—shapes, areas, distances, directions—and accepts the distortion of the rest. [2] The cost is a severe inflation of areas toward the poles, so that Greenland appears comparable in size to Africa, which is in fact some fourteen times larger. [3] Critics have argued that such distortions shape, below the level of conscious belief, how people perceive the relative importance of regions. [4] Equal-area projections have been promoted as a corrective, though they in turn distort shapes, stretching the continents near the equator into unfamiliar forms. The controversy is sometimes presented as a contest between a false map and a true one. It is better understood as a reminder that every map embodies a purpose, and that a projection suited to steering ships is not thereby suited to comparing the extent of nations.",
