"p": "If every event is determined by prior causes, our choices appear to be links in a chain that began long before we were born. [1] Many conclude that freedom is an illusion, since we could never have done otherwise than we did. [2] Compatibilists reply that this conclusion rests on a mistaken idea of what freedom requires. [3] What distinguishes a free act from an unfree one, they argue, is not the absence of causes but the kind of causes at work: an act is free when it issues from the agent’s own reasons and desires rather than from coercion, compulsion, or manipulation. [4] Critics object that the distinction merely relocates the problem, since the agent’s reasons and desires were themselves produced by causes he did not choose. Compatibilists answer that no coherent notion of freedom could require us to have authored ourselves from nothing. The freedom worth wanting, on their view, is the capacity to act on one’s own considered reasons, a capacity that determinism leaves untouched.",
