"p": "Until the middle of the nineteenth century, each town kept its own time, setting its clocks by the passage of the sun overhead, so that noon in a western town came several minutes after noon in an eastern one. [1] The discrepancies were harmless while travel was slow, since no traveller could move between towns fast enough to be inconvenienced by them. [2] The companies responded by imposing a uniform time across their networks, and for some decades towns kept two times, the local and the railway, sometimes displayed on the same clock face. [3] The eventual adoption of standard time by law, and of international time zones in the 1880s, ratified what commercial necessity had already established. [4] Historians have seen in the episode more than an administrative convenience. The hour, which had been a local and astronomical fact, became a national and conventional one, set by institutions rather than by the sky. That shift prepared the way for forms of coordination—factory shifts, broadcast schedules, synchronised markets—that presuppose that everyone’s clocks agree.",
