"p": "The rotation speeds of stars in the outer regions of spiral galaxies are far higher than the gravitational pull of the visible matter in those galaxies can account for, and they stay high far out from the centre. [1] The dominant interpretation infers an additional, invisible mass—dark matter—distributed in a halo around each galaxy. [2] Its proponents point to the independent evidence that supports the inference, from the bending of light by galaxy clusters to the pattern of fluctuations in the cosmic microwave background. [3] Yet decades of increasingly sensitive experiments designed to detect dark-matter particles directly have found nothing. [4] Such modifications reproduce galaxy rotation curves with striking economy, often from the visible matter alone. They have struggled, however, to account for the behaviour of clusters and for the cosmic background, which is where dark matter performs best. Each approach thus succeeds where the other is weakest, a situation that some philosophers of science regard as a reason to keep both research programmes alive rather than declare a winner prematurely.",
