"p": "Much of the software on which the internet depends was written by programmers who gave it away, often without payment and sometimes without even public credit. [1] Studies of contributors found the motives less mysterious than theory implied. [2] Many programmers wrote code to solve their own problems and lost nothing by sharing the solution; others valued the reputation that visible contributions earned with potential employers; still others simply enjoyed the work. [3] Firms, meanwhile, discovered that sharing the costs of common infrastructure with competitors was cheaper than each maintaining its own, and began paying employees to contribute. [4] The result is an unusual hybrid, in which volunteers and salaried engineers collaborate on projects owned by no one and used by everyone. Its vulnerabilities have become apparent in recent years, as critical components turn out to be maintained by a handful of unpaid individuals. The free-rider problem, dissolved at the level of motivation, has reappeared at the level of maintenance, where dull and essential work attracts neither reputation nor pleasure.",
