"p": "A carbon offset allows an emitter to compensate for its emissions by paying for a reduction elsewhere, for instance by financing the protection of a forest that would otherwise have been cleared. [1] In principle the arrangement is efficient, since it directs money to wherever reductions are cheapest. [2] In practice its integrity depends on a counterfactual that can never be observed: what would have happened without the payment. [3] Such cases are not hypothetical: projects have strong incentives to exaggerate the threat, and investigations of several large forest schemes have found baselines of projected deforestation inflated well beyond any plausible trend. [4] Even genuine reductions may prove temporary, since a protected forest can later burn or be logged, releasing the carbon it was credited with storing. For these reasons many climate scientists argue that offsets should supplement rather than substitute for direct cuts. The deeper difficulty is that an offset converts an uncertain estimate into a certain-seeming number, and the precision of the number conceals the uncertainty from which it was derived.",
