"p": "The great encyclopaedic museums of Europe and North America defend their collections with an argument about access: by gathering objects from many cultures under one roof, they allow visitors to see each in relation to all the others. [1] The argument has force, but it is notably silent about how the objects came to be under that roof. [2] To the communities from which objects were taken by force, the appeal to universal access sounds less like a principle than a justification after the fact, offered by those who benefited from the taking. [3] They add that the objects are not merely specimens of a culture but parts of it, with ritual, political, or ancestral significance that display in a foreign gallery cannot honour. [4] Several institutions have begun to return contested objects or to negotiate arrangements in which ownership is transferred while loans allow the objects to remain on view. Such arrangements suggest that the choice between keeping and returning may be less absolute than either side once assumed. They also suggest that the universal museum’s strongest argument, access, can be served by circulation as well as by possession.",
