"p": "Many of the most admired Victorian novels first appeared not as books but in monthly or weekly parts, often written only a few instalments ahead of publication. [1] The mode of publication left its marks on the form: chapters ending in suspense, a profusion of memorable minor characters who could be recognised after a month’s absence, and several plots interwoven to sustain interest. [2] Later critics, judging the novels as finished wholes, often found them baggy and episodic, their coincidences contrived and their structures loose. [3] The judgement assumed that a novel is properly an object designed in advance and contemplated entire, an assumption that serial publication did not share. [4] Its looseness was in part the price of that responsiveness, and in part the means by which a story could remain open to the reactions of its audience. To read such a novel as a failed attempt at organic unity is to judge it by a standard formed by a different mode of production. Recent criticism has accordingly attended to the original parts, with their advertisements and illustrations, as the form in which the work was actually experienced.",
