"p": "The word meritocracy was coined in the 1950s by a sociologist who intended it as a warning rather than an ideal. [1] The oppression he foresaw would be psychological rather than legal, lying in what such a society would do to the self-understanding of its members. [2] Those at the top, believing their position fully earned, would feel no obligation to those below, while those at the bottom would be denied even the consolation that their failure was due to bad luck or injustice. [3] Within a generation the word had lost its irony, and politicians across the spectrum used it as a term of unqualified praise. [4] Recent critics argue that the warning was prophetic, pointing to the contempt that credentialed elites can display toward those without degrees, and to the resentment it provokes. Others reply that the alternative to meritocracy is not equality but patronage, and that the remedy for its excesses is to broaden the definition of merit rather than abandon it. Both sides agree, at least, that a society cannot sort people by achievement without also shaping how they regard themselves and one another.",
