"p": "The modern civil service, recruited by examination and promoted by rule, was created in many countries to replace a system of patronage in which public offices were rewards for political loyalty. [1] Reformers argued that officials appointed by merit and protected from dismissal would serve the state rather than a party, and would bring expertise rather than gratitude to their work. [2] Bureaucracies accordingly acquired a reputation for caution, rigidity, and devotion to procedure, qualities that are rational for an official who is rarely rewarded for initiative and rarely punished for inaction. [3] Successive waves of reform have tried to restore responsiveness through performance targets, short-term contracts, and political appointments at senior levels. [4] Each measure, however, reintroduces some of the dangers the original reform was meant to remove, since an official whose career depends on pleasing ministers is less able to tell them unwelcome truths. The history of administrative reform thus resembles a pendulum swinging between the vices of patronage and the vices of insulation. The most durable arrangements appear to be those that combine security of tenure with transparency, exposing officials to public scrutiny rather than to the favour of particular superiors.",
