"p": "Orchestras of the eighteenth century were usually directed from within, by a keyboard player or the leading violinist, who kept time while playing his own part. [1] As ensembles grew larger and scores more complex in the following century, coordination required a figure who stood apart from the players and directed them with a baton. [2] The new figure was at first conceived as a servant of the score, whose task was merely to set tempos, cue entries, and hold together forces that could no longer hear one another clearly. [3] Within a few decades conductors were being credited with readings of the works they directed, and audiences learned to distinguish one conductor’s Beethoven from another’s. [4] The development raised a question that has never been settled: whose music is being heard, the composer’s or the conductor’s? Advocates of historically informed performance answered by reviving smaller ensembles and older practices, in part to diminish the conductor’s interpretive authority. Their critics replied that every performance is an interpretation, and that the choice is not between a mediated and an unmediated work but between different mediators.",
