"title": "The Progress Problem",
"p": [
"It has become a commonplace, among philosophers no less than their detractors, that philosophy does not progress. The questions Plato pressed — what justice requires, whether knowledge is possible, how mind stands to matter — are still set for undergraduates, and surveys of the profession record not convergence but stable, almost ritual, division. Measured against the natural sciences, where yesterday’s controversy becomes tomorrow’s textbook, the discipline looks less like an inquiry than a conversation conducted for its own sake.",
"The standard rejoinder is that philosophy’s victories are concealed by their own success. Whenever a question becomes tractable — the motion of bodies, the structure of inference, the mechanisms of perception — it is annexed by a new science and ceases to be called philosophy; the discipline is then indicted for the residue it is left holding. The rejoinder has the charm of reversing the charge, but it purchases that charm at a price. It concedes the critic’s criterion — that progress consists in the delivery of answers — and merely redirects the credit; and because any persistent failure can be redescribed as a question not yet ripe for export, it is immune to disconfirmation in a way that ought to embarrass a discipline that prides itself on rigour.",
"A more interesting reply contests the criterion itself. Much of what philosophers achieve is eliminative: they show that two attractive theses cannot be held together, that an argument trades on an ambiguity, or that what seemed one question is in fact several. After Edmund Gettier’s three-page paper of 1963, the analysis of knowledge as justified true belief became untenable for almost everyone, though nothing has replaced it by general consent. Such results are not answers, but they are not nothing either: the space of defensible positions has contracted, and the costs of each surviving position have been priced.",
"Even this reply, however, proves less than its proponents hope. A discipline that advances chiefly by subtraction must explain why the subtraction never seems to terminate — why, after two millennia, the surviving positions remain as numerous as ever, merely more sophisticated. It is possible that the eliminations are real but are matched by a steady invention of new options, so that the map is redrawn rather than filled in. If so, the right description of philosophy’s achievement is not knowledge acquired but understanding deepened: a better grasp of why the questions resist settlement and what any settlement would have to accommodate. Whether this counts as progress is not a further fact to be discovered but a matter of what one took philosophy to be for. Those who wanted a verdict will remain unconsoled; those who wanted to see the shape of a difficulty clearly have been, on the whole, rather well served."
],
