"title": "The Discipline of the Unhappened",
"p": [
"Few methodological prohibitions are as widely observed, or as rarely examined, as the historian’s suspicion of the counterfactual. E. H. Carr’s dismissal of ‘might-have-beens’ as a parlour game expressed a professional consensus with respectable foundations: the archive testifies only to what occurred, and a claim about what would have occurred under other conditions seems to lie beyond the reach of any document. Popular speculation about worlds in which the Armada landed or the Confederacy prevailed has done little to dispel the suspicion, since such exercises tend to reveal more about their authors’ allegiances than about the past.",
"The difficulty is that the prohibition cannot be obeyed. Every causal attribution conceals a counterfactual: to say that the failed harvest of 1788 helped bring about the Revolution is to assert that, absent the failure, the course of events would have differed in some material respect. Historians who disdain counterfactual argument do not thereby escape it; they merely leave it unstated, and an unstated premise is precisely the kind that escapes scrutiny. The professional taboo, on this view, does not secure rigour but disguises a dependence it cannot acknowledge. Even the most austere archival monograph, when it ranks causes by importance, is quietly weighing worlds that never existed.",
"It does not follow that all counterfactuals are equally admissible. The ones worth entertaining alter a single circumstance that contemporaries themselves regarded as open — a closely contested vote, an illness that might have been survived, a letter that nearly went astray — and trace consequences only so far as the evidence about actors’ dispositions and constraints can carry them. Beyond that horizon, possibilities ramify faster than they can be disciplined, and the historian is no longer reconstructing the past but composing fiction about it. The question of Napoleon victorious at Waterloo fails not because it is counterfactual but because nothing in the record can constrain an answer to it.",
"There is a subtler argument for the disciplined counterfactual, one that its critics rarely confront. Narrative, the historian’s native form, is retrospective by construction: it is written from the vantage of an outcome, and it arranges antecedents as though they had been leading there all along. The effect is to convert contingency into destiny without anyone having argued that it should be so. To reconstruct the options that agents actually faced — and to take seriously that some of them nearly prevailed — is to restore to the past the openness it had for those who lived it. The counterfactual, so understood, is not an indulgence at the margins of historical method but a corrective to a bias at its centre; the irony is that the profession’s caution about imagined pasts has left it peculiarly exposed to an imagined necessity."
],
