"title": "The Author’s Afterlife",
"p": [
"For much of the twentieth century, the most respectable thing a literary critic could do with an author was to ignore him. W. K. Wimsatt and Monroe Beardsley’s 1946 essay on ‘the intentional fallacy’ argued that a poem’s meaning is public, embodied in its language and available to any competent reader, so that appeals to what the poet privately meant are either redundant, when the text already shows it, or illegitimate, when it does not. Two decades later Roland Barthes gave the same exclusion a more apocalyptic cast: the author, he declared, was dead, and the birth of the reader was to be paid for with that death. Where the New Critics had wanted to protect the text from biography, Barthes wanted to free it from any single origin that might arrest the play of its meanings.",
"The two positions, often treated as allies, rest on incompatible premises. Wimsatt and Beardsley assumed that a text possesses a determinate meaning which careful reading can recover; Barthes denied precisely this. What united them was a shared hostility to a figure — the author as proprietor of meaning — rather than a shared theory of what meaning is. The alliance was tactical, and like most tactical alliances it dissolved once the common enemy seemed defeated.",
"It was the question both camps had left unasked — what makes marks meaningful in the first place — that Steven Knapp and Walter Benn Michaels pressed in ‘Against Theory’ in 1982. Imagine, they proposed, that marks on a beach spell out a stanza of Wordsworth, and suppose we then discover that they were produced by the wash of the waves. Have we found a poem? Their answer was that we have not: marks made by no one are not language at all, merely shapes that resemble it, and so to read anything as meaningful is already to treat it as intended. On this view the dispute over whether to consult intention is confused, since there is no meaning prior to intention to which one could appeal instead.",
"The argument is more powerful against Barthes than its critics allow and less useful to practising readers than its authors implied. If meaning presupposes intention, the free play of an authorless text is not liberation but a category error. Yet to establish that meaning is intended is not to establish how intention is to be known, and on that question Knapp and Michaels have nothing to add to the old resources of philology, context and attentive reading — the very resources the New Critics recommended. The author thus survives the reports of his death, but in a curiously diminished form: not as an authority to be consulted, but as the condition that makes consultation of the text intelligible at all."
],
