"title": "What the Forger Steals",
"p": [
"When Han van Meegeren, charged after the Second World War with having sold a national treasure to Hermann Göring, saved himself by proving that the ‘Vermeer’ in question was his own work, he posed a question that aesthetics has never quite answered. His forgeries had been authenticated by the foremost connoisseurs of the day; The Supper at Emmaus had been greeted as a masterpiece on its acquisition by the Boijmans museum. Once the attribution collapsed, the same canvases came to seem leaden and obvious. If nothing in the paint had changed, what exactly had been lost?",
"The simplest answer is that nothing had, and that the collapse of the forgeries’ reputation measures only the snobbery of a market that prices signatures rather than surfaces. The answer has the appeal of demystification, but it rests on an assumption that will not bear much weight: that the aesthetic properties of a picture are fixed by what can be seen in it at a given moment. Nelson Goodman pointed out that this cannot be right even on its own perceptual terms. To know that one of two apparently identical pictures is a forgery is to have a reason to look at them differently, and looking differently is how discriminations not presently available come to be made. The knowledge does not alter what is there to be seen; it alters what the trained eye will eventually see — and indeed later viewers have found van Meegeren’s debts to the taste of the 1930s hard to miss.",
"Goodman’s argument preserves the primacy of perception while denying that perception is static. Denis Dutton went further, arguing that artworks are apprehended not merely as objects but as performances — as the outcome of an agent’s solving a problem under particular constraints. To admire a Vermeer is in part to admire what it was to have painted it, in that place and moment, with those means. A forgery offers the result without the achievement, much as a flawless recording that proves to have been spliced together note by note offers the sound of a virtuoso performance that never took place.",
"The two accounts are complementary rather than rival, and together they expose what the demystifying view misdescribes. The connoisseurs who turned against the forgeries were partly nursing wounded pride, and the sudden ugliness they discovered owed something to embarrassment. But they were not simply confessing that they had been looking at labels. What they had admired was never a surface alone; it was a surface understood as a particular kind of act. The forger’s offence is not that he counterfeits beauty — he may, for a while, achieve it — but that he counterfeits a history, and invites us to admire an achievement that no one performed."
],
