"The word ‘meritocracy’ entered English as an accusation. Michael Young’s The Rise of the Meritocracy (1958) imagined a Britain in which intelligence plus effort had become the sole principle of social placement, and it ended with the lower orders, now deprived of any excuse for their position, rising against the very fairness that had sorted them. Within a generation the satire had been read as a manifesto. The term now names an aspiration shared across the political spectrum, and Young, late in life, complained that his warning had been mistaken for a recommendation.",
