"p": [
"Modern democracies take for granted that democracy means elections. Classical political thought assumed nearly the opposite. For Aristotle, the allocation of offices by lot was characteristically democratic and their allocation by election characteristically oligarchic, since voters invited to choose the best will tend to choose the distinguished — the wealthy, the eloquent, the well connected. Athens filled most of its magistracies and its council by lottery, reserving election for posts, such as the generalships, in which expertise was thought indispensable. Bernard Manin has argued that the founders of modern representative government knew this tradition and chose election deliberately, not as a means of realising popular rule but as a means of tempering it: representatives would resemble the people in being chosen by them, but differ from them in being, as a rule, their social superiors.",
"This genealogy has encouraged a revival of sortition. Citizens’ assemblies, drawn by lot and stratified to mirror the population, have deliberated on electoral reform in British Columbia, on constitutional questions in Ireland and on climate policy in France. Their advocates observe that a randomly selected body is descriptively representative to a degree no parliament approaches, and that its members, owing nothing to party or donor, can change their minds — a liberty that elected representatives, bound to manifestos and to the next campaign, can seldom afford.",
"The objections are serious. A lottery confers no mandate; its members cannot be punished at the polls for bad judgments, and they bear no responsibility for implementing what they propose. Their deliberations are shaped by organisers who select the experts, frame the questions and set the agenda, so that the apparent voice of the people may conceal the steering hand of a technocracy. And a body of a hundred, however representative in composition, cannot claim the authorisation of the millions who did not choose it. There is also the awkward fact that those who agree to serve are not a random sample of those invited, so that the lottery’s claim to mirror the population is weaker in practice than in principle.",
"Yet these objections tell chiefly against a proposal few serious advocates now make: the substitution of sortition for election. Against the more modest arrangement that the Irish case exemplifies — an assembly that recommends, while a legislature or a referendum decides — they lose much of their force. The irony is that the most persuasive case for the lottery today is not the Athenian one. It is not that sortition is democratic and election oligarchic, but that each corrects a characteristic failure of the other: election supplies accountability and authorisation, while the lottery supplies the deliberative freedom that electoral competition erodes. Whether that division of labour succeeds depends less on the principle of selection than on unglamorous matters of design — who chooses the experts, and whether governments are bound to answer recommendations they would prefer to ignore."
],
