"In 1944 a German woman, wishing to be rid of her husband, reported to the authorities remarks he had made disparaging Hitler. He was tried under wartime statutes and sentenced to death, though he was eventually sent to the front instead. After the war she was prosecuted, and the court that convicted her was widely reported to have held that the statutes on which she relied were contrary to the sound conscience of all decent human beings. The ‘grudge informer’ case became the occasion, in 1958, of a celebrated exchange between H. L. A. Hart and Lon Fuller on the relation between law and morality.",
"Hart, defending legal positivism, insisted that the existence of a law is one question and its merit another. A wicked statute validly enacted is still law; to deny this is to confuse what the law is with what it ought to be. Yet Hart was no apologist for the informer. If she was to be punished, he argued, it would be more honest to enact a retroactive statute and to acknowledge that a lesser evil was being chosen — the sacrifice of the principle against retroactive punishment — than to pretend that the Nazi statutes had never been law. Positivism, on his account, is not moral indifference but moral candour: it keeps the question of obedience open by refusing to settle it by definition.",
"Fuller replied that Hart’s separation could not be sustained. Law, he argued, has an internal morality — generality, promulgation, non-retroactivity, clarity, a congruence between the rules declared and their administration — and a regime that systematically violates these requirements does not produce bad law so much as fail to produce law at all. The Nazi state, with its secret decrees and its courts that ignored even its own statutes when convenient, had departed so far from these conditions that its enactments could not command the fidelity owed to law. On this view Hart’s distinction between law as it is and law as it ought to be is not false but premature, since some measure of the ought is already built into the is.",
"It is tempting to score the debate as a victory for one side, but the more instructive outcome is that each position turned out to need the other’s insight. Hart’s candour depends on a confidence that legal validity can be identified without moral argument, a confidence the Nazi case strains, since what made the regime’s ‘law’ so unlike law was precisely its abandonment of the procedural virtues Fuller described. Fuller’s procedural morality, conversely, is compatible with considerable substantive injustice: a regime may promulgate clear, general, prospective rules and administer them faithfully while those rules are monstrous. Fuller showed that law is not morally empty; Hart showed that it is not morally sufficient. The informer case resists resolution because it sits exactly where those two truths meet."
