"Paulo Freire’s Pedagogy of the Oppressed (1968) supplied progressive education with its most durable image of what it opposed. In the ‘banking’ model, the teacher deposits information into students conceived as empty accounts; they receive, file and store, and the more completely they accept their passive role, the better they are judged to have learned. Against this, Freire proposed a problem-posing education in which teacher and students investigate together the conditions of their own lives, and in which literacy is acquired not as a neutral skill but as a means of naming, and thereby contesting, the world. His literacy campaigns in north-eastern Brazil, in which reading was taught through words charged with local significance, seemed to show that the method worked.",
"The image has proved more influential than the argument. In the decades since, ‘banking’ has become a term of abuse for any instruction in which a teacher transmits knowledge, and the transmission of knowledge itself has come under suspicion. Critics from the opposite camp, such as E. D. Hirsch, have argued that the suspicion is self-defeating. Reading comprehension, on the evidence of cognitive psychology, depends heavily on background knowledge; a student who lacks the references a text assumes cannot read it critically, because she cannot read it at all. Those who arrive at school already possessing such knowledge acquire more of it at home, and a pedagogy that declines to teach it explicitly leaves its distribution to the accidents of birth — the very inequality progressive education set out to undo.",
"The critique is powerful, but it is aimed more accurately at Freire’s heirs than at Freire. His objection was never to knowledge as such but to a relationship in which knowledge is treated as the teacher’s possession, delivered to students whose own experience counts for nothing. The peasant who learns to read ‘slum’ and ‘work’ is not being spared content; he is being given content of a particular kind, selected for its bearing on his situation. Freire’s quarrel is with the question of who selects, and on whose behalf — a question that a curriculum of shared cultural reference, however egalitarian in intent, tends to answer by appeal to the canon of those already in charge.",
"The two positions thus answer different questions, and each is weakest where it pretends to answer the other’s. A pedagogy of critical consciousness that does not furnish knowledge will produce critics without materials; a pedagogy of knowledge that does not ask whose knowledge it furnishes will produce competence without the means to question its own premises. The opposition between deposit and dialogue is, on this view, less a choice between methods than a reminder that every curriculum is at once a transfer and a selection, and that pretending it is only one of these is the characteristic evasion of each camp. The irony is that each side, in caricaturing the other, reproduces the very fault it diagnoses: the progressive withholds the knowledge that critique requires, and the traditionalist forgets that a canon is also an argument."
