"Proposals to cool the planet by injecting reflective aerosols into the stratosphere, an idea whose natural precedent is the eruption of Mount Pinatubo in 1991, which lowered global mean temperatures by roughly half a degree for more than a year, have met an objection that is moral before it is technical. To research such schemes, critics argue, is to supply governments with an excuse for not reducing emissions: the mere prospect of a cheap remedy will erode the resolve that costly mitigation requires. Borrowing a term from the economics of insurance, they call this moral hazard.",
"Defenders of research usually reply on the critics’ own empirical ground. The handful of studies that have examined how people react to information about geoengineering have yielded mixed results, and several found that exposure heightened rather than diminished concern about warming, perhaps because so drastic a remedy signalled the gravity of the disease. If the public is not lulled, the defenders conclude, the hazard is speculative.",
