"That early detection saves lives has the status less of a hypothesis than of a moral axiom, and screening programmes are commonly defended by pointing to the improved five-year survival of patients whose cancers were found early. Yet survival statistics of this kind are structurally incapable of settling the question they are invoked to answer. If a test merely advances the date of diagnosis without altering the date of death, survival measured from diagnosis lengthens by exactly the interval gained, an artefact epidemiologists call lead-time bias. More insidiously, if a test detects lesions that would never have progressed to cause symptoms, each such lesion enters the statistics as a cancer whose patient survives, inflating survival rates while altering nothing about the course of anyone’s life.",
"This second phenomenon, overdiagnosis, is not error in the ordinary sense. It is distinct from the false positive, in which a test signals disease where there is none; the overdiagnosed lesion satisfies every pathological criterion for malignancy. What is mistaken is not the diagnosis but the prognosis tacitly attached to it. The clearest population-level illustration comes from South Korea, where the spread of inexpensive thyroid ultrasound after 1999 was followed by an approximately fifteen-fold rise in recorded thyroid cancer by 2011, while mortality from the disease remained essentially flat, a pattern explicable only if the additional diagnoses were overwhelmingly of tumours that would not have killed.",
"Defenders of screening respond, reasonably, that such cases are unrepresentative and that for several cancers randomised trials demonstrate reductions in disease-specific mortality. The concession should be granted, but its scope noted. Disease-specific mortality depends on attributing each death to a cause, and deaths from the complications of treatment, a surgical misadventure or a cardiac event following radiotherapy, may be recorded under other headings. Trials large enough to detect differences in all-cause mortality would require populations far larger than any yet enrolled, so the most decisive evidence is precisely the evidence that cannot practically be obtained.",
