"title": "Within the Rules of the Game",
"p": [
"In 1970 Milton Friedman argued that the social responsibility of a business is to increase its profits, so long as it stays within the rules of the game, and for half a century the doctrine of shareholder primacy served as the working constitution of the Anglo-American corporation. When nearly two hundred chief executives of large American companies signed a statement in 2019 committing their firms to serve customers, employees, suppliers and communities as well as shareholders, the gesture was widely read as that constitution’s repeal. Critics of primacy welcomed it as overdue; its defenders warned that it would license managers to pursue whatever ends they pleased under the cover of a public purpose.",
"The defenders’ warning deserves to be taken more seriously than their rhetoric usually allows. A manager instructed to maximise a single quantity can be judged against it; a manager instructed to balance the interests of several constituencies, with no rule for trading one against another, can justify almost any decision by invoking whichever constituency it happens to favour. Accountability to everyone, on this argument, collapses into accountability to no one, and the chief beneficiaries of the collapse are managers themselves. There is some evidence for the worry: subsequent studies found that few signatories had altered their governance documents or tied executive pay to stakeholder outcomes, suggesting that the commitment was cheaper to make than to keep.",
"Yet the case for primacy rests on a premise that its advocates state less often than they should. Friedman’s formula was conditional: profit is to be pursued ‘within the rules of the game’, and the moral adequacy of the doctrine depends on those rules being complete enough that what is profitable is also, broadly, socially tolerable. Where harms can be priced, regulated or litigated, the condition plausibly holds. Where they cannot, because they are diffuse, delayed, borne by people without standing to sue, or concealed by the very firms that cause them, maximising profit within the rules may mean maximising the harms the rules fail to reach. Moreover, firms are not passive recipients of the rules; they lobby to shape them, which makes the condition partly endogenous to the behaviour it is meant to constrain.",
"The dispute is therefore less a clash of values than a disagreement about an empirical question that neither side tends to pose explicitly: how complete, in a given industry, are the rules of the game? Where they are reasonably complete, the accountability argument for primacy is strong and stakeholder rhetoric is likely to serve managerial convenience. Where they are badly incomplete, primacy loses its moral warrant, but the remedy implied is better rules, not vaguer mandates, since a corporate duty to everyone does little to supply the enforcement whose absence created the problem."
],
