"The widening of a congested motorway is among the most reliably popular and reliably disappointing of public works. Within a few years of new lanes opening, traffic speeds typically return to their former levels, a pattern so regular that transport economists have given it the status of a law. Studying American metropolitan areas, Gilles Duranton and Matthew Turner found that vehicle-kilometres travelled on urban interstates rose roughly in proportion to lane-kilometres built: add ten per cent to capacity and, in time, about ten per cent more driving fills it. The mechanism is unmysterious. Congestion suppresses trips, some of which are retimed, rerouted or abandoned, and new capacity releases them, along with the development that cheaper access invites, until delay once again deters the marginal traveller.",
"Critics of road-building have adopted the finding as a verdict: capacity expansion, they say, is futile, and the money would be better spent on public transport. Both halves of that conclusion are less secure than they appear. A widened road that fills up again is carrying more people than before, and the additional journeys presumably had value to those who made them; the project has failed only by the standard of its own advertising, which promised faster travel rather than more of it. Whether the extra trips justify the expense, once their emissions and the sprawl they encourage are counted, is a question the fundamental law does not answer.",
"The second half fares worse. The same logic that defeats road widening applies to any measure that frees road space without changing what it costs to use it. If a new rail line persuades some drivers to leave their cars at home, the space they vacate is quickly claimed by others whose trips had previously been deterred, and congestion returns to its equilibrium level. Duranton and Turner’s data are consistent with this: they found little evidence that expanding public transport reduced the amount of driving in a city. Transit may be valuable for other reasons, since it moves people who cannot drive and supports denser settlement, but as a remedy for road congestion it is subject to the very law invoked on its behalf.",
"What breaks the law is price. A road free at the point of use is rationed by queuing whenever demand exceeds capacity, and congestion is simply the queue made visible. Charging drivers for the use of scarce road space at busy times, as Singapore and Stockholm do, alters the equilibrium itself rather than the capacity at which it is reached. The objection that such charges burden poorer commuters is serious but not decisive, since queuing is a regressive rationing device too: it taxes time, and the time of those with inflexible working hours is least easily rearranged."
