"title": "When the Brain Refuses Our Categories",
"p": [
"Functional neuroimaging has supplied the most recognisable icon of modern brain science: a grey cortex mottled with patches of colour marking the regions said to ‘light up’ during a task. Outside the laboratory these images have licensed a striking kind of claim, that consumers love their phones because a region associated with love is active when they see them, or that a voter’s amygdala betrays a fear of a candidate. The inference involved runs from observed activity to a mental state, and methodologists have long called it reverse inference, to distinguish it from the forward inference, from manipulated task to measured activity, that experiments are designed to support.",
"The standard critique is that reverse inference is valid only to the extent that a region is selective. If the amygdala were active during fear and nothing else, its activity would indicate fear; but it responds also to novelty, to faces, to reward and to mere salience, so its activation is weak evidence for any one of them. Russell Poldrack formalised the point in probabilistic terms: the strength of a reverse inference depends on how much more likely the activation is given the mental process than given its absence, and for many popular regions the ratio is modest. To this logical problem critics add a statistical one. A scan tests many thousands of small volumes simultaneously, and without correction some will cross a threshold by chance, as researchers demonstrated by recording apparent ‘activity’ in a dead salmon shown photographs of human social situations.",
"Both criticisms are sound, and the second has largely been absorbed into standard practice. But the first is frequently pressed beyond its warrant. Poldrack’s analysis does not show reverse inference to be a fallacy; it shows it to be a probabilistic inference whose strength varies, and large databases of published studies now allow that strength to be estimated rather than assumed. More interestingly, low selectivity admits two readings. It may mean that a region participates in many distinct processes. It may instead mean that the processes, as psychology currently individuates them, do not correspond to anything the brain treats as a unit: that ‘fear’, ‘reward’ and ‘salience’ are partly overlapping descriptions of a single underlying function that has yet to be named.",
"On the second reading, the failure of reverse inference is not merely a limit on what imaging can tell us about the mind; it is evidence about the mind itself, a sign that the categories inherited from folk psychology may need revision. That possibility cuts against both the enthusiasts, who read brain images through those categories uncritically, and the sceptics, who treat the categories as fixed and fault the images for failing to map onto them. Imaging data cannot settle the matter alone, but they may be better suited to reforming our taxonomy of mental processes than to confirming it."
],
