"In The Coal Question (1865), William Stanley Jevons advanced a claim that has unsettled energy policy ever since. The improving efficiency of steam engines, he observed, had not reduced Britain’s consumption of coal but multiplied it, since cheaper motive power made profitable a host of applications that had not previously been worth pursuing. To suppose that the economical use of fuel implied a diminished consumption of it was, in his judgement, a confusion. The proposition that efficiency can raise total demand, now called the Jevons paradox, is invoked today by sceptics of efficiency standards for appliances, vehicles and buildings, who argue that such measures are at best futile and at worst self-defeating.",
"Economists prefer the more neutral term ‘rebound’. When an appliance delivers the same service with less energy, the effective price of the service falls, and users respond partly by consuming more of it; this direct rebound recovers some fraction of the engineering saving. Money saved may also be spent on other energy-using goods, an indirect rebound, and at the scale of whole economies cheaper energy services can shift the composition of output itself. Jevons’s claim is that the sum of these effects can exceed one hundred per cent of the expected saving, a case termed ‘backfire’. The empirical difficulty is that the counterfactual, the economy as it would have been without the efficiency gain, is never observed, so that direct rebounds can be estimated with some confidence while economy-wide ones remain largely a matter of modelling.",
"Where measurement is feasible, direct rebounds for household services in wealthy countries appear real but well short of backfire; people drive somewhat more in efficient cars, but demand for mobility and warmth is bounded by the hours in a day and the temperature at which a room becomes comfortable. Jevons’s coal differed in kind. Steam power was not a final service but a general-purpose input to an industrialising economy, and its cheapening opened entire sectors whose existence the original engineering calculation could not have anticipated. Backfire is most plausible precisely where efficiency lowers the cost of an input whose potential uses are many and far from saturated.",
"The paradox, then, is neither the refutation of efficiency policy its sceptics claim nor the curiosity its advocates suppose, but a conditional whose antecedent must be examined case by case. Computation may be the clearest contemporary candidate: the energy required per operation has fallen by many orders of magnitude, while the uses of computation have multiplied, most recently with the data centres built for artificial intelligence. For mature final services, efficiency remains among the cheapest means of reducing emissions. For general-purpose inputs, it should be expected to stimulate growth, and any intended limit on total consumption will have to come from prices or caps rather than from engineering."
